   "finding_id": "gap1-1",
   "title": "[R4:gap1-1] Strategy-2 bare substring containment picks a superstring-titled sibling — or an entirely different owned record — as the Discogs play-count write target, and misses exactly-owned records on decorated Shazam titles",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `gap1-1` · **Wave 1 — Credit the right record (matching & attribution)** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **HIGH**_\n\n**Location:** `src/metadata/discogs/reader.py:149`  \n**Lens:** Gap: Identification-to-attribution matching semantics (wrong-record credit / silently missed album completion)\n\n## Defect\nsearch_collection Strategy 2 matches with `album_lower in title and any(artist_lower in a for a in artists)` (reader.py:149), iterating the index in most-recently-added-first order (lines 144-146), and the winner's instance_id becomes the Discogs Play Count / Last Played write target. Bare substring containment has four failure modes, all demonstrated through the real search_collection: (1) SUPERSTRING SIBLING: 'led zeppelin ii' is a substring of 'led zeppelin iii', so a user who owns BOTH — with III added more recently — gets II's plays written to III's instance, even though II is exactly owned. (2) SELF-TITLED FAMILIES: all four Peter Gabriel albums are titled 'Peter Gabriel' on Discogs; the match silently picks the most-recently-added owned one. (3) CROSS-ARTIST: 'war' ⊂ 'warpaint' for both artist and title, so playing War's 'War' (not owned) writes a full play to an owned Warpaint 'Warpaint'. (4) The Discogs disambiguation suffix ('Nirvana (2)') passes the containment test for a DIFFERENT band of the same name, widening class 3. The SEC-1 fix (#82) guards only empty/whitespace terms — these are all legitimate, non-degenerate strings. The containment is also one-directional, so the common decorated-Shazam album ('Rumours (Deluxe Edition)') MISSES an exactly-owned 'Rumours' — the test only ever 'works' in the collision-prone short-string direction.\n\n## Failure scenario\nUser owns Led Zeppelin II and III, III added to the collection later. He plays II; its pressing is not among the top-25 database candidates (Strategy 1 misses — the exact case Strategy 2 exists for per its own docstring, 'catches rare/obscure pressings'). Strategy 2 iterates added-desc, hits III first because 'led zeppelin ii' ⊂ 'led zeppelin iii', and at session end increment_play_count/update_last_played land on III's instance_id: II's play history stays flat, III's is corrupted — silently, forever. Same class as SEC-1 (#82, severity:high) with realistic instead of degenerate inputs.\n\n## Suggested fix\nReplace containment with tiered exact-first matching on normalized strings (casefold + NFKC + strip the Discogs ' (n)' disambiguation suffix from index artist names): tier 1 exact album+artist equality; tier 2 a conservative bounded fuzzy (token-set ratio or startswith-with-word-boundary) only if it produces a UNIQUE match across the whole index — on ambiguity (two entries qualify) return None, per the SEC-1 refuse-to-guess principle, so the track degrades to the database tier (no write target) instead of guessing one.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe tiered exact-first fix is sound; refinements: (1) strip the Discogs ' (n)' disambiguation suffix from INDEX ARTIST names only (Discogs applies it to artist names, not titles) before tier-1 equality; (2) for tier 2, normalizing the SHAZAM album by dropping a trailing parenthetical ('(Deluxe Edition)', '(Remastered)') before an exact-equality retry is safer and cheaper than token-set ratio, and directly fixes the Case-4 miss without reintroducing containment; (3) keep the uniqueness requirement — on two qualifying index entries return None (SEC-1 refuse-to-guess), which also resolves the Peter Gabriel self-titled family to a safe no-write rather than an arbitrary credit; (4) the existing SEC-1 empty-term guard stays (tier logic makes it redundant for matching but it still short-circuits the index build cost and keeps the documented log line); (5) add regression tests pinning all five repro cases, including the reverse-direction miss.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nreader.py:149: `if album_lower in title and any(artist_lower in a for a in artists):`; lines 144-145: \"dict iteration is insertion order = collection 'added desc', so on a substring collision the most-recently-added owned album wins\". Executed via the real search_collection (only _collection_index/_database_search/_build_result faked), /tmp/repro_strategy2.py: Case 1 -> \"Shazam: artist='Led Zeppelin' album='Led Zeppelin II' -> matched release_id=5001 instance_id=91 ... EXPECTED instance 92 (the owned II); GOT instance 91 (WRONG RECORD)\"; Case 3 -> \"artist='War' album='War' (user does NOT own it) -> matched {'release_id': 7001, 'instance_id': 55}\" (owned Warpaint); Case 4 -> \"album='Rumours (Deluxe Edition)'; owned title='Rumours' -> None\"; Case 5 -> \"'nirvana' in 'nirvana (2)' = True\".\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
