  {
   "finding_id": "gap1-2",
   "title": "[R4:gap1-2] SideIndex.from_tracklist locates the track by exact lower().strip() equality — routine Shazam-vs-Discogs title divergence (remaster/live/feat suffixes, ’ vs ', NFD accents) silently forfeits the album-completion Play Count and blanks t…",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `gap1-2` · **Wave 1 — Credit the right record (matching & attribution)** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **HIGH**_\n\n**Location:** `src/metadata/models.py:114`  \n**Lens:** Gap: Identification-to-attribution matching semantics (wrong-record credit / silently missed album completion)\n\n## Defect\nThe current track is located in the Discogs tracklist by `e.title.lower().strip() == title_key` (models.py:114-115) with ZERO further normalization — grep confirms no unicodedata/casefold/decoration-stripping anywhere on this path (the only normalization in the codebase, recognizer.py:400, serves _same_track dedup and never touches this comparison). The title is Shazam's verbatim string (resolver.py:189 passes raw.title; recognizer.py:160/197 passes track['title'] through untouched), and Shazam's Apple-Music-backed catalogue systematically decorates titles that vinyl Discogs tracklists carry plain: ' - 2011 Remastered Version', '(Remastered 2009)', '(feat. X)', '(Live)', '(Mono)', typographic ’ vs ', NFD vs NFC accents, '…' vs '...', 'Pt. 2' vs 'Part Two'. Any mismatch yields SideIndex.empty: is_last_track is False, so PlaySession.potential_last_track (models.py:343) never arms and ListenTracker takes the 'Last track not reached' branch (listen_tracker.py:347-351) — the Play Count, Last Played, AND the Last.fm love are all silently skipped even when every track of the album identified perfectly. On a 12-pair corpus of realistic divergence forms driven through the real class, 10/12 missed. The failure is doubly silent: the log says 'Last track not reached (likely only one side played)', which is FALSE, and the display simultaneously loses the side caption ('B1', 'NN OF MM') and prev/next titles for the affected track, so the operator's only clue is a subtly emptier card.\n\n## Failure scenario\nUser plays The Dark Side of the Moon start to finish; every track confirms. Shazam returns the closer as 'Eclipse - 2011 Remastered Version' (the catalogue entry it matched); the Discogs pressing's tracklist row is 'Eclipse'. Equality fails -> SideIndex.empty -> is_last_track False -> potential_last_track never arms -> no Play Count, no Last Played, no love — for a flawlessly identified complete playthrough, with a log line blaming the listener ('likely only one side played'). Repeats every time that pressing is played: an unrecoverable, systematic under-count for every album whose Shazam catalogue titles are decorated.\n\n## Suggested fix\nIntroduce one shared normalize_title() used by from_tracklist on BOTH sides of the comparison: unicodedata.normalize('NFKC', s).casefold(), map typographic apostrophes/quotes/ellipsis/dashes to ASCII, collapse whitespace, then strip trailing decoration — parenthetical/dash suffixes matching (remaster|remastered|live|mono|stereo|feat\\.?|single version|\\d{4} mix...). Try exact normalized equality first, then decoration-stripped equality; keep the existing conservative failure (empty) only when both tiers miss. Add the 12-pair corpus as a regression test.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe tiered fix is right in shape but needs three corrections. (1) There are THREE comparison sites in from_tracklist sharing title_key semantics — models.py:115 (current), :146 (side-ordinal loop), and the global-index position+title match near :179 — plus the docstring-documented invariant that the first side entry matching the title IS current (MUT-16 removal relied on it); all sites must use the same normalize helper or side_position/global_index desync. (2) Tier 2 (decoration stripping) must only accept a UNIQUE match across the tracklist — stripping '(Live)' conflates 'Song' and 'Song (Live)' when both appear on one release (live album with studio bonus), which would resurrect the phantom-last-track class META-4/#78 closed; keep SideIndex.empty on ambiguity. (3) Tier 1 (NFKC + casefold + punctuation folding + whitespace collapse) is lossless and should be unconditional; do NOT fold it into recognizer.py:400's _same_track normalizer — that one intentionally compares Shazam-to-Shazam and needs no decoration logic. Add the corpus as regression tests including the two adversarial duplicate-title cases.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nmodels.py:111-115: `title_key = title.lower().strip()` ... `current = next((e for e in tracklist if e.title.lower().strip() == title_key), None)`. grep for unicodedata|casefold|normalize across src/ finds only recognizer.py:400 (_same_track dedup, not this path). Executed /tmp/repro_sideindex.py through the real SideIndex: \"remaster suffix matched=False is_last_track=False track_display=''\" (likewise remaster-2, feat., typographic apostrophe, NFD accents, &-vs-and, Pt./Part, live, ellipsis, mono) — \"2/12 matched; MISSED ... 10 forms\"; consequence check: closer identified as 'Eclipse - 2011 Remastered Version' vs tracklist 'Eclipse' -> is_last_track=False.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
