  {
   "finding_id": "data-3",
   "title": "[R4:data-3] Last.fm love targets last identified track, not the closer that armed it — replay or FALLBACK swap after the closer loves the wrong track/artist (executed repro)",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `data-3` · **Wave 1 — Credit the right record (matching & attribution)** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **MEDIUM**_\n\n**Location:** `src/tracking/listen_tracker.py:365`  \n**Lens:** Data integrity: Discogs writes, scrobble, sessions\n\n## Defect\nThe module contract (listen_tracker.py:9: 'Calls LastFmClient.love on the last track') and the code comment (line 353: 'love the last track if the full side completed') mean the album's closing track — the track whose `is_last_track` armed `potential_last_track`. But `_finalize_session` recomputes the love target as `session.identified_tracks[-1]` (line 365): the last track IDENTIFIED in the session, which equals the closer only if nothing was identified after it. Two realistic sequences break that: (1) the user finishes the album and re-drops side A within the 45s silence window — same release id, so no album split, and the replayed opener is appended after the closer; (2) the user swaps in a record that resolves as FALLBACK (no discogs_release_id) — the split never triggers for release-less tracks (listen_tracker.py:553-557 requires both ids), so the new record's tracks are appended to the old session. On session end the Play Count credit correctly goes to album A, but the love is written to the operator's real Last.fm profile against the wrong track — in case (2) a track of a DIFFERENT record/artist entirely. B-23 (#72) covered double-love idempotency; no closed issue covers wrong-target love.\n\n## Failure scenario\nAlbum A plays through 'Closer' (potential_last_track=True). User lifts the needle and re-drops side A 20s later; 'Opener' is identified and logged into the same session. Needle lift, 45s silence, session ends: Discogs credits album A (correct), then `last_track = session.identified_tracks[-1]` = 'Opener' and Last.fm loves 'Opener' instead of 'Closer'. Executed repro (real PlaySession + real ListenTracker, fake writer/lastfm): output shows `potential_last_track=True identified_tracks=['Closer', 'Opener']` → `LASTFM : love -> Band A — 'Opener'` → `INFO ✅ Last.fm loved: Band A — Opener`.\n\n## Suggested fix\nRecord the arming track on the session: in PlaySession.log_track, `if track.is_last_track: self.potential_last_track = True; self.closing_track = track`. In _finalize_session, love `session.closing_track` (falling back to identified_tracks[-1] only if unset).\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nFix is sound and minimal. Concretely: add `closing_track: Optional[TrackMetadata] = None` to the PlaySession dataclass (src/metadata/models.py), set it in log_track inside the existing `if track.is_last_track:` branch (after the append; the consecutive-dedup early-return is harmless because the first occurrence already set it), and in _finalize_session use `last_track = session.closing_track or (session.identified_tracks[-1] if session.identified_tracks else None)`. The fallback keeps behavior identical for old/edge sessions. Also update the line-353 comment and consider a regression test mirroring both repro sequences (replay-after-closer and FALLBACK swap) asserting the love target is the closer.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nlisten_tracker.py:365: `last_track = session.identified_tracks[-1] if session.identified_tracks else None` — inside the love branch gated on potential_last_track. models.py:342-344 (log_track): `self.identified_tracks.append(track); if track.is_last_track: self.potential_last_track = True` — the arming track is not remembered. Executed output of /tmp/repro_wrong_love.py: \"identified_tracks=['Closer', 'Opener'] ... LASTFM : love -> Band A — 'Opener' ... EXPECTED love target: 'Closer'\".\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
