  {
   "finding_id": "gap1-3",
   "title": "[R4:gap1-3] Shazam attribution swing to an owned release whose tracklist ends with the playing track mints a one-track split-off session that phantom-credits that record's Play Count",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `gap1-3` · **Wave 1 — Credit the right record (matching & attribution)** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **MEDIUM**_\n\n**Location:** `src/tracking/listen_tracker.py:553`  \n**Lens:** Gap: Identification-to-attribution matching semantics (wrong-record credit / silently missed album completion)\n\n## Defect\nThe album-change auto-split (listen_tracker.py:553-570) treats ANY confirmed release-id change as a physical record swap, and PlaySession.log_track arms potential_last_track from any track's is_last_track with no check that the track belongs to the latched album (models.py:343-344). Compose the two with a routine Shazam behaviour — per-track album attribution swinging to a compilation for a hit single (each track resolves independently under the resolver's per-(artist,album)-string cache, resolver.py:76) — and a straight-through play of Album X can credit a never-touched record: the swung track resolves to the user's owned 'Greatest Hits' (release 200, own instance_id), triggers split #1, becomes the sole track of a new session where it latches the compilation AND arms potential_last_track (chronological compilations routinely end with the major hit), and the next Album-X track triggers split #2, finalizing that one-track session -> increment_play_count(200, comp_instance). Executed through the real ListenTracker/PlaySession/TrackMetadata (only the writer faked): one continuous play of Album X issued TWO increments — one for X (correct) and one phantom full-album credit for the compilation. The closed split work (#2 B-2, #23 B-8, v1.3.4/v1.3.5 docstrings) addresses double-crediting the SAME session/release; none addresses a misattribution-minted session whose only track is another owned record's closer. Distinct from the already-found same-release re-drop merge (equal ids, undercount) — this is unequal ids, overcount, wrong record.\n\n## Failure scenario\nUser owns 'Album X' and 'Greatest Hits' (which closes with 'The Hit'). He plays Album X start to finish. Shazam attributes the mid-album 'The Hit' to 'Greatest Hits'; the resolver's collection tier finds the owned compilation. Result: Album X's Play Count +1 (correct) AND Greatest Hits' Play Count +1 plus a fresh Last Played date — for a record that never left its sleeve. The compilation's listening history is permanently corrupted and, with love_on_completion, the phantom session's track is also love-eligible.\n\n## Suggested fix\nArm the completion gate per-release rather than per-session: in log_track, set potential_last_track only when track.discogs_release_id matches the session's latched album_release_id (or when both will latch together from this track). Additionally consider requiring a minimum of 2 identified tracks (or a minimum session age) before a split-off/needle-lift session is credit-eligible, so a single misattributed track can never constitute a 'completed album'; log the suppression so a deliberate drop-the-needle-on-the-closer play is diagnosable.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe PRIMARY suggested fix is a NO-OP for the demonstrated defect and should be dropped: the album-change split already guarantees a session never contains two release ids (any differing-id track triggers a split and lands in a fresh session), so cross-release arming within a session cannot occur — and in the reproduced scenario the swung track latches release 200 AND arms potential_last_track from the SAME log_track call, which the fix's own \"(or when both will latch together from this track)\" clause explicitly admits. I re-checked: applying that gate changes nothing in the repro. The SECONDARY suggestion is the real fix: make a split-off (or any) session credit-ineligible unless it identified at least one track of the latched release BEFORE the closer (equivalently >=2 identified tracks of that release), logging the suppression. Tradeoff to surface to Lane: today a deliberate needle-drop on just the closer credits a full-album play (the conservative-by-design docstring only covers the inverse), and this gate would suppress that; given the app's data-integrity posture (missed count preferred over phantom count, per the META-4 precedent), suppression-with-log is consistent, but it is a behavior change requiring sign-off. A minimum-session-age gate is weaker (a real closer-only play and the phantom both last one track) and adds a clock dependency; prefer the track-count/same-release-predecessor gate.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\n**⚠ Correction (report verification pass):** the finder transcript below could NOT have executed as written — `comp_track.is_last_track = True` assigns to a read-only property. The defect is nonetheless CONFIRMED: the verifier reproduced it independently through the real ListenTracker/PlaySession/TrackMetadata/SideIndex with `is_last_track` computed from real tracklists. Finder transcript retained, struck through where invalid, for the record.\n\nlisten_tracker.py:553-557 splits on `track.discogs_release_id != self._session.last_release_id`; models.py:343-344: `if track.is_last_track: self.potential_last_track = True` (no release check); listen_tracker.py:591 finalizes the split-off session. Executed /tmp/repro_phantom_compilation.py through the real classes: \"~~comp_track.is_last_track = True~~ ... Discogs writes issued: [('increment_play_count', 200, 20), ('increment_play_count', 100, 10)] PHANTOM full-album Play Count for the never-played compilation (release 200 / instance 20)\".\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
