  {
   "finding_id": "gap1-4",
   "title": "[R4:gap1-4] Strategy 1 accepts the first owned release among 25 loose-search candidates with no title cross-check — a similar-titled owned album can become the instance_id write target",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `gap1-4` · **Wave 1 — Credit the right record (matching & attribution)** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **MEDIUM**_\n\n**Location:** `src/metadata/discogs/reader.py:127`  \n**Lens:** Gap: Identification-to-attribution matching semantics (wrong-record credit / silently missed album completion)\n\n## Defect\nStrategy 1 fetches up to 25 candidates from `self._client.search(album, artist=artist, type='release')` (reader.py:253-254) and returns the FIRST whose release.id is in the collection index (lines 127-134) — ownership of the candidate is the ONLY criterion; the candidate's title is never compared against the recognized album, and ordering is delegated entirely to Discogs free-text relevance ranking (a q= search, not an exact-title filter — Discogs matches loosely, so 'Greatest Hits' surfaces 'Greatest Hits II', deluxe/anniversary editions, and similarly-titled releases by the same artist inside the first page). There is no ordering guarantee that pressings of the recognized album precede other owned matches. So a wrong-but-owned release that Discogs ranks within the top 25 — while the correct owned pressing ranks below 25 or isn't owned at all — becomes the instance_id write target. This is labeled a hypothesis in one respect: the wrong outcome depends on live Discogs ranking, which cannot be executed offline; the code-side semantics (first-owned-wins, zero title validation) are quoted below and are not in dispute. Mildest form: the user owns two pressings of the same album and the credit lands on whichever the search ranks first (wrong copy, same album); severe form: playing an unowned 'Greatest Hits' credits the owned 'Greatest Hits II'.\n\n## Failure scenario\nUser owns Queen 'Greatest Hits II' but not 'Greatest Hits'; plays a borrowed 'Greatest Hits'. _database_search('Queen', 'Greatest Hits') returns GH and GH II pressings interleaved by relevance; the first candidate found in the index is a GH II pressing (the only owned one present). Strategy 1 returns it with GH II's instance_id, and the session-long album cache (resolver.py:133) pins every track of the play to it — Play Count and Last Played land on GH II. No log distinguishes this from a correct hit.\n\n## Suggested fix\nAfter `entry = index.get(release.id)` hits, validate the candidate before accepting: normalized (per finding 2's normalize_title) comparison of release.title against the recognized album (Discogs search-result titles are 'Artist - Album', so compare the album segment or use containment on the normalized pair); on mismatch keep scanning, and if only mismatching owned candidates exist, fall through to Strategy 2 / return None rather than writing to a release whose title doesn't correspond to what was recognized. Alternatively prefer, among owned candidates, the one with the best title match instead of the first.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe suggested Strategy-1 fix is incomplete on its own: my executed repro also shows Strategy 2's substring containment (reader.py line 149, `album_lower in title`) accepts the same wrong album — 'greatest hits' is a substring of 'greatest hits ii' — so falling through from a title-mismatched Strategy 1 just re-credits the identical wrong record via Strategy 2. Both strategies need the same normalized-title rule (equality or best-match, with containment demoted to tie-breaker at most). Two implementation cautions: (1) Discogs search-result titles are 'Artist - Album', so split on the first ' - ' or use suffix matching before comparing; (2) exact-equality-only would regress legitimate matches where recognition returns a slightly different album string than the Discogs title (deluxe/remaster suffixes on the OWNED correct release), so prefer 'best normalized match among owned candidates' over 'reject on any mismatch' to avoid trading wrong-writes for lost scrobbles on owned records.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nreader.py:126-134: `candidates = self._database_search(artist, album, limit=25)` / `for release in candidates:` / `entry = index.get(release.id)` / `if entry is not None: ... return self._build_result(release, instance_id=entry['instance_id'])` — no comparison of release.title to `album` anywhere in the loop. reader.py:253: `results = self._client.search(album, artist=artist, type='release')` — a free-text q= search whose ranking the code does not constrain. The docstring's own claim of order (line 93-94, 'Returns the first hit') documents first-hit-wins without any exactness guarantee.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
