  {
   "finding_id": "gap3-1",
   "title": "[R4:gap3-1] B-4's uncached DB-tier carve-out revokes the album-split detector's documented same-release-id premise: one transient blip on an album's first resolve causes a spurious split, and in the closer-first case silently loses the Play Count …",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `gap3-1` · **Wave 1 — Credit the right record (matching & attribution)** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **MEDIUM**_\n\n**Location:** `src/metadata/resolver.py:158`  \n**Lens:** Gap: Closed-issue regression sweep: do the 178 closures still hold in v1.5.7?\n\n## Defect\nThe B-4/#19 remediation deliberately returns a DISCOGS_DATABASE result *uncached* when the collection tier raises transiently (resolver.py:156-158: 'Return it for this track, but leave it uncached so the next track retries the collection lookup'). But the v1.3.4/v1.3.5 album-split detector (listen_tracker.py:553-557) fires whenever consecutive tracks carry different release IDs, and its soundness rests on a premise stated in the module docstring (listen_tracker.py:26-28): 'The signal is reliable because MetadataResolver's album cache (v1.3.3) guarantees every track of an album resolves to identical release IDs within a session.' B-4's carve-out is precisely the case where that guarantee does not hold: the database tier's best-match pressing (search_database, limit=3, first buildable candidate) is routinely a *different release id* than the user's owned pressing (strategy 1 cross-references up to 25 candidates against the collection index). So a single transient Discogs error (429, network blip — or any of the errors is_transient misclassifies) on the album's first resolve makes track 1 carry the DB pressing id and track 2 the collection pressing id, and the tracker logs 'Album change detected mid-session (release 222 → 111) — splitting session' for the same physical record. In the normal play order this is session fragmentation and log noise (the credit still lands). But when the session's first identified track is the album closer (needle dropped on the last track, then more of the album plays — a resume/replay pattern), the split detaches the session that held potential_last_track=True before any collection-sourced track could latch album_release_id/instance_id, and the successor session never sees the closer again (the album cache now holds the collection result, and the closer already played). The credit is lost — where WITHOUT the split, B-4's own recovery design would have credited it (the later collection-resolved track latches the release/instance pair into the same still-potential_last_track session). This is a Round-1 remediation (B-4) silently weakening an earlier shipped guarantee (the v1.3.4/1.3.5 split), the exact class this lens hunts.\n\n## Failure scenario\nUser drops the needle on the closer of an owned record (resuming side B's end / replaying a favorite closer), then lets more of the album play. The closer's resolve hits one transient Discogs error on the collection tier → resolves DB-tier (different pressing id, is_last_track=True, no instance_id). The next track resolves collection-tier → split fires → the closer's session is finalized with 'Last track reached but release not in Discogs collection — skipping Play Count' and the new session ends with 'Last track not reached'. increment_play_count is never called; without the blip (or without the spurious split) it would have been. Additionally, ANY normal full-album play whose first resolve blips produces a spurious 'Album change detected' split of a single physical record.\n\n## Suggested fix\nMake the split detector tolerant of tier-mismatch on the same album: when the differing release ids resolve from the same normalized (artist, album) cache key — or when the incoming track's source is DISCOGS_DATABASE/FALLBACK and the session's latched album matches on (artist, album) — treat it as the same album (update last_release_id, don't split). Alternatively, have resolve() remember the transiently-failed key for the session and suppress the split when the previous track's id came from an uncached degraded resolve. Also correct the listen_tracker.py:26-28 docstring premise, which is stated as a guarantee B-4 revoked.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe suggested direction (tolerate tier-mismatch on the same album) is right, with two corrections. First, don't match on TrackMetadata.album: _from_discogs sets album from the Discogs result (discogs_result.get(\"album\")), so the DB pressing and the owned pressing can carry different album strings (\"… (Remastered)\"); match on the resolver's normalized cache key (raw Shazam artist/album) — which means threading the raw key or the normalized pair onto TrackMetadata, or having resolve() expose 'this key resolved degraded' state the tracker can consult. Second, the cheapest safe rule is asymmetric: suppress the split only when the PREVIOUS last_release_id came from a DISCOGS_DATABASE-sourced track AND the incoming track is COLLECTION-sourced with matching normalized (artist, album) — i.e. a tier upgrade of the same album — and then update last_release_id and let the latch proceed; a genuine record swap still splits because the artist/album pair changes. (Requires recording the source alongside last_release_id in PlaySession — one extra field.) Minor residual to note in the fix: the DB-sourced closer's is_last_track was computed against the DB pressing's tracklist, which can differ from the owned pressing's; acceptable, since it errs toward the existing conservative behavior. And regardless of mechanism, the listen_tracker.py:26-30 docstring must be corrected — it currently states a guarantee (identical release IDs within a session) that B-4 deliberately revoked.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nresolver.py:156-158: 'if discogs_completed: self._cache_store(...)  return self._from_discogs(raw, result, MetadataSource.DISCOGS_DATABASE)' — the uncached-degraded return. listen_tracker.py:553-557 splits on 'track.discogs_release_id != self._session.last_release_id'. Executed repro (/tmp/repro_split2.py, real MetadataResolver + real ListenTracker, only the reader/writer stubbed; one ConnectionError on the first collection lookup, DB pressing id 222 vs collection 111): V1 (normal order) prints 'Album change detected mid-session (release 222 → 111) — splitting session' for one physical record (credit still lands). V2 (closer-first) prints 'Last track reached but release not in Discogs collection — skipping Play Count and Last Played updates' then 'Last track not reached' — '>>> Play Count increment called: False'. Re-running V2 with no blip (control): increment called True.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
