  {
   "finding_id": "data-4",
   "title": "[R4:data-4] Same-release re-drop within the 45s silence window merges two complete playthroughs into one session — Play Count undercounts by one (split blind to equal release ids)",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `data-4` · **Wave 1 — Credit the right record (matching & attribution)** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **LOW**_\n\n**Location:** `src/tracking/listen_tracker.py:553`  \n**Lens:** Data integrity: Discogs writes, scrobble, sessions\n\n## Defect\nThe v1.3.4 album-change auto-split exists precisely so that sessions merged by a fast record change don't mis-credit plays, but its only trigger is `track.discogs_release_id != self._session.last_release_id` (listen_tracker.py:553-557). Re-dropping the SAME record (or replaying a side containing the closer) within the 45s silence window produces equal release ids, so no split fires: both complete playthroughs share one PlaySession, `potential_last_track` is already latched, and `_credit_completed_album` runs once at session end. The second completed play is silently uncounted. The signal needed to detect this is already in the session: a newly identified track of the same release arriving AFTER potential_last_track was armed (e.g. the opener, global_index 0) is a replay boundary. This is arguably within the 'conservative by design' philosophy, so I rank it LOW — but the hunt question 'the SAME album played twice in a row' has a concrete undercount answer, and no closed issue (#3 B-3 dedup, #78 META-4, #163) addresses the same-release merge.\n\n## Failure scenario\nUser plays album A to the end, immediately lifts and re-drops it (a 20-30s flip, well under session_end_silence_seconds=45), plays it through again, then stops. Silence → SESSION_ENDED → one session with potential_last_track=True → exactly one Play Count increment. Two complete listens, one credit; Last Played is correct but the count permanently undercounts by one.\n\n## Suggested fix\nIn on_track_identified (under the lifecycle lock), treat 'session.potential_last_track is True AND the incoming track has the same release id AND is not itself the closer (or has a global_index earlier than the last logged track)' as a replay boundary: detach-and-restart exactly like the release-change split, deferring the detached session's finalize outside the lock. Alternatively, document the undercount as accepted in the module docstring.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe proposed replay-boundary split is workable and its main apparent misfire is benign: a stale earlier-index track of the same release arriving after the closer (overlapping-chunk re-identification) would split and credit immediately — but that credit is deserved (the closer did play), and the resulting fresh session ends uncredited at the true silence, so net behavior stays correct. Two details to get right: (1) the incoming replay track must be logged into the NEW session, which the existing split path already does (log_track runs after _start_session at listen_tracker.py:572 — the current code handles this for free); (2) exclude the closer itself as the boundary trigger only when it is a consecutive identical re-identification (the models.py dedup already swallows that case before the check would run, so in practice \"same release + potential_last_track armed + not consecutive-dup\" suffices; an explicit global_index-earlier condition is stricter and also fine, but FALLBACK tracks without a release id must keep not triggering). The documented-as-accepted alternative is equally defensible given the module's stated conservative philosophy — but it must actually be written into the docstring, since the current \"conservative by design\" paragraph covers only the Side-A-only case.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nlisten_tracker.py:553-557: `if (self._session.last_release_id is not None and track.discogs_release_id is not None and track.discogs_release_id != self._session.last_release_id):` — the sole split trigger; equal ids can never split. models.py:343-344: `if track.is_last_track: self.potential_last_track = True` — never reset, so the merged double-play session credits exactly once via _finalize_session's single `_credit_completed_album` call (listen_tracker.py:306-323).\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
