  {
   "finding_id": "data-1",
   "title": "[R4:data-1] #163 finalize retry re-runs the full read-modify-write, double-crediting Play Count when a POST outcome is ambiguous (applied but response lost)",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `data-1` · **Wave 2 — Write-path integrity & the error taxonomy** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **HIGH**_\n\n**Location:** `src/tracking/listen_tracker.py:430`  \n**Lens:** Data integrity: Discogs writes, scrobble, sessions\n\n## Defect\nThe bounded finalize retry added by #163 (`_finalize_write_with_retry`, listen_tracker.py:406-417) retries by calling `writer.increment_play_count` from scratch each attempt (listen_tracker.py:430-437). Each attempt performs the FULL read-modify-write: it re-reads the current field value (`_get_field_value`, writer.py:110) and POSTs `current+1`. The transport's own 429 retry was carefully made idempotent by re-POSTing the SAME absolute value (writer.py:148-152, transport.py:196), but the outer #163 retry was not given the same treatment. Any AMBIGUOUS failure — a 15s read timeout after Discogs already processed the POST, a connection reset mid-response, a proxy 502 after apply — is swallowed by `increment_play_count`'s `except Exception` (writer.py:165-167) and returned as False, so the retry re-reads the ALREADY-INCREMENTED value and increments it again. One completed play then adds +2 (or +3) to the operator's real Discogs collection, with a success log ('Play Count updated ... 6 → 7'). B-8 (#23) fixed re-entrancy idempotency and B-15 (#30) fixed the 429 retry, but this is a new compositional defect introduced by the #163 fix itself; no closed issue covers retry-after-ambiguous-failure.\n\n## Failure scenario\nPlay count is 5. Album completes; SESSION_ENDED finalize runs. Attempt 1: GET reads '5', POST {value:'6'} — Discogs applies the write but the response is lost (requests.Timeout after 15s on flaky Pi Wi-Fi). increment_play_count returns False; retry wrapper sleeps 1s. Attempt 2: GET reads '6' (the applied write), POSTs '7' → 204. Final count 7 for one play, logged as success. Executed repro (real ListenTracker + real DiscogsCollectionWriter, only the HTTP seam faked): BEFORE play_count=5 → 'Failed to increment Play Count: read timed out' → 'Play Count updated ... 6 → 7' → AFTER play_count=7, DOUBLE INCREMENT.\n\n## Suggested fix\nMake the retried unit idempotent, mirroring the 429 path: resolve field_id and read the current value ONCE (retrying only the GET, which is safe), compute new_count, then have _finalize_write_with_retry retry ONLY the absolute POST of that fixed new_count. E.g. split writer API into read_play_count(release, instance) and set_play_count(release, instance, value); ListenTracker computes value once and retries only set_play_count.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe suggested split (read_play_count once, then retry only the absolute set_play_count) is correct and mirrors the proven B-15 pattern — a re-POST of the same absolute value is idempotent whether or not the lost first POST was applied. Three refinements: (1) the single read must keep the META-1/_READ_FAILED semantics — if the GET fails (it may itself be bounded-retried, GETs are safe), abort the whole credit without writing, leaving `credited` False as today; (2) scope the change to the Play Count path only — the other user of _finalize_write_with_retry (Last.fm love, listen_tracker.py:~370) is naturally idempotent per-track and needs no split, and update_last_played is already an absolute set and deliberately single-attempt (STAB-2); (3) update the #163 tests, which currently pin \"fails twice then succeeds → committed\" against a wholesale-mocked writer — add a seam-level test (fake session.post applying then raising, as in this verification) so the applied-but-unobserved case is pinned RED-first, per the repo's reproduce-the-loss rule. Residual accepted risk worth stating in the fix: if the response of the FINAL retry is also lost, the play was credited server-side but `credited` stays False and the loud \"credit LOST\" log will be wrong in the other direction (says lost, actually landed) — with no read-back verification that ambiguity is irreducible; a follow-up could re-read the value on ambiguous failure to disambiguate.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nlisten_tracker.py:430-437: `success = await self._finalize_write_with_retry(\"Discogs Play Count increment\", lambda: self.writer.run(self.writer.increment_play_count, session.album_release_id, session.album_instance_id))` — a fresh full read-modify-write per attempt. writer.py:110: `raw_value = self._get_field_value(release_id, instance_id, field_id)` runs inside every attempt. Executed output of /tmp/repro_double_increment.py: \"BEFORE: server play_count = 5 ... ERROR Failed to increment Play Count for release 123: read timed out (after server applied write) ... INFO Play Count updated for release 123 / instance 777: 6 → 7. ... AFTER : server play_count = 7 (POSTs applied by server: 2) ... DOUBLE INCREMENT\". Note tests/test_listen_tracker.py:354 pins exactly this 'fails twice then succeeds → committed' pattern without modeling the applied-but-unobserved case.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
   "labels": [
    "code-review",
    "bug",
    "severity:high",
    "data-integrity"
   ],
   "milestone_title": "Wave 2 — Write-path integrity & the error taxonomy"
  },
  {
   "finding_id": "data-2",
   "title": "[R4:data-2] Shutdown cancels an album-split credit mid-write: the inline finalize in the recognition leg is cancelled before tracker.drain(), which only covers _bg_tasks — silent half-credit (CONC-1 class, split path)",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `data-2` · **Wave 2 — Write-path integrity & the error taxonomy** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **MEDIUM**_\n\n**Location:** `src/tracking/listen_tracker.py:592`  \n**Lens:** Data integrity: Discogs writes, scrobble, sessions\n\n## Defect\nCONC-1 (#76) added `tracker.drain()` so an in-flight end-of-session credit is not torn in half by loop teardown — but drain() only awaits `_bg_tasks` (listen_tracker.py:177), which holds ONLY the fire-and-forget SESSION_ENDED tasks created in on_silence_event (listen_tracker.py:120-121). The album-split credit takes a different path: on_track_identified awaits `_finalize_detached(detached)` INLINE (listen_tracker.py:592), inside TrackCommitService.commit, inside RecognitionLoop.run() — which is a pipeline leg. run_pipeline cancels all pending legs (`t.cancel()` + gather, main.py:232-234) BEFORE the finally block runs `await tracker.drain()` (main.py:255). CancelledError is a BaseException, so `_finalize_write_with_retry`'s `except Exception` does not contain it: the split credit is killed at whatever await it is on — after increment_play_count landed but before update_last_played, or before `credited` latches at all. The #163 retry window (up to ~3×(15s HTTP + backoff) ≈ 50s when Discogs is flaky) makes the exposure window much wider than a single write. This is exactly the CONC-1/CONC-3 defect class, resurfacing on the split path that #163/#166 later built.\n\n## Failure scenario\nRecord A finishes (closer identified); user swaps in record B within 45s. B's first confirmed track triggers the split; the recognition leg is now inline-awaiting the retrying Discogs credit for A (Discogs is slow — attempt 1 timed out, wrapper sleeping before attempt 2). Operator presses ESC / systemd sends SIGTERM / any other leg faults → run_pipeline's FIRST_COMPLETED fires, `t.cancel()` cancels the recognition leg, CancelledError lands inside the finalize. drain() then finds `_bg_tasks` empty and returns immediately. Outcome: A's Play Count possibly incremented server-side but `credited` unlatched and Last Played never written (half-credit), or the credit lost entirely — silently, with the CONC-1 protection reporting nothing.\n\n## Suggested fix\nRoute the split-path credit through the same machinery as SESSION_ENDED: in on_track_identified, wrap the creditable finalize in `task = asyncio.create_task(self._finalize_detached(detached)); self._bg_tasks.add(task); task.add_done_callback(self._on_end_session_done)` and then `await asyncio.shield(task)` (or just await it, accepting that a cancelled commit leaves the task running for drain() to await). drain() then covers both paths.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe suggested fix's second variant is the right one: create_task + `self._bg_tasks.add(task)` + `task.add_done_callback(self._on_end_session_done)`, then PLAIN `await task` — not asyncio.shield. Cancelling a coroutine that is awaiting a task does not cancel the awaited task, so on shutdown the inline await unwinds while the registered credit task keeps running for drain() to await; shield adds nothing once the task is registered. Plain await also preserves two behaviors callers depend on: LB-1 (a finalize raise still propagates out of on_track_identified so current_raw stays un-advanced, track_commit_service.py:126-131) and the #166 short-circuit (only creditable splits reach this, so no new lock traffic). _on_end_session_done already handles the double-retrieval (awaiter raises AND callback logs) and checks task.cancelled() first, so it composes cleanly. One check for the implementer: the split credit now competes for the same _SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_SECONDS=10 drain budget as a concurrent SESSION_ENDED credit — the #163 backoff (1s+2s) plus writer HTTP time fits, but verify against the writer's actual per-request timeout as the drain comment assumes.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nlisten_tracker.py:591-592: `if detached is not None and detached.potential_last_track: await self._finalize_detached(detached)` — awaited inline, never registered in _bg_tasks. listen_tracker.py:177: `pending = [t for t in self._bg_tasks if not t.done()]` — drain sees only on_silence_event tasks. main.py:231-234: `for t in pending: t.cancel(); await asyncio.gather(*pending, return_exceptions=True)` runs before the finally's `await tracker.drain()` (main.py:255). Call chain verified: recognizer.py:365 `await self._handle_result(result, epoch)` → on_confirmed → commit → on_track_identified, all inside the recognition pipeline leg.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
   "labels": [
    "code-review",
