  },
  {
   "finding_id": "err-1",
   "title": "[R4:err-1] Transient 429/network error during _build_result's lazy release fetch is swallowed per-field and the degraded result (no cover, empty tracklist) is cached session-long as a Discogs hit, silently forfeiting the album's Play Count / Last …",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `err-1` · **Wave 2 — Write-path integrity & the error taxonomy** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **MEDIUM**_\n\n**Location:** `src/metadata/discogs/reader.py:365`  \n**Lens:** Error handling, resilience & cross-fix interaction\n\n## Defect\nThe B-4/B-13 invariant says a transient failure must leave an album uncached/retryable, and errors.py exists to enforce it at the resolve boundary. But _build_result (reader.py:342-420) sits one layer BELOW that boundary and swallows exceptions per-field ('except Exception: pass' at lines 365-366, 377-378, 390-391, 402-408; get_tracklist returns [] on any error at 205-207). Its docstring defends this as graceful degradation of 'missing or malformed' optional data — but the release objects handed to it from strategy 1 of search_collection (reader.py:134) and from search_database are PARTIAL search-result objects: discogs_client lazily refresh()es on the first access of any field absent from the search payload (images, labels, styles, master — verified in the installed library: fetch() -> refresh() -> client._get(), which raises discogs_client.exceptions.HTTPError on any non-2xx). That fetcher is NOT routed through DiscogsHttp, so it has no 429 retry (transport.py:96-98 documents this). So a single transient 429/connection blip during that lazy fetch lands in the per-field excepts and yields a result with cover_art_url=None, tracklist=[], label/catno/genres empty — and the resolver then CACHES that dict as a DISCOGS_COLLECTION (resolver.py:133) or DISCOGS_DATABASE (resolver.py:157) hit, with no TTL, for the whole session. Because tracklist=[] makes SideIndex.empty() and is_last_track=False for every track (models.py:108-109, 190) — and is_last_track is 'the sole gate on Discogs play-count updates' (models.py:249-250) — the album's closer never sets potential_last_track, so Play Count, Last Played, AND the Last.fm love are all silently skipped at session end ('Last track not reached', listen_tracker.py:348-351). This is a composition bug between two prior fixes: A-6's 'intentional defensive extraction' (#10) and the B-4/B-13 no-caching-on-transient rule (#19/#28) — each individually correct, unsafe composed, exactly the standing attack surface requested.\n\n## Failure scenario\nFirst track of a collection-owned LP resolves; the database search succeeds, the collection index matches, then a momentary wifi blip or a 429 (plausible right after a multi-page index build on a large collection consumed rate budget) hits the lazy /releases/{id} refresh inside _build_result. Only a WARNING about the tracklist appears (the images/labels/master failures are fully silent). Every track of that album for the rest of the session (process restarts daily) renders with no cover art, no year/label/catalog, no PREV/NEXT — and when the record finishes, the completed play earns no Play Count, no Last Played, no love, logged as the routine 'Last track not reached (likely only one side played)'.\n\n## Suggested fix\nForce the full release fetch ONCE at a boundary that is allowed to raise — e.g. call release.refresh() (or access one lazy field un-caught) at the top of _build_result before the per-field extraction, so a transport error propagates to the resolver's transient handler and the album stays uncached/retryable; the per-field excepts then really do cover only missing/malformed data as documented. Alternatively, catch TRANSIENT_EXTERNAL_ERRORS specifically inside the per-field guards and re-raise them (mirroring coverart.py's #175 pattern), and treat a get_tracklist transient failure the same way since tracklist gates the Play Count credit.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe forced-refresh fix is sound and net-zero on request count (all three call sites pass never-refreshed objects, so the up-front refresh() replaces the implicit lazy one); it can additionally SAVE a request by reusing the now-loaded release.tracklist instead of get_tracklist's second full GET of the same release (reader.py:394 -> 191). But the fix as suggested is incomplete: search_database (reader.py:175-182) catches Exception from _build_result and continues to the next candidate, so with all candidates failing transiently it returns None WITHOUT raising, discogs_completed stays True, and resolver.py:171-172 caches FALLBACK — the identical session-long pin, one tier down. That per-candidate catch must re-raise transient errors (is_transient / TRANSIENT_EXTERNAL_ERRORS) or the bug just moves. Also, if the alternative per-field-reraise route is taken instead, get_original_year's \"except Exception: master_id = None\" (reader.py:222-228) must be included, since release.master is another lazy-fetch swallow of the same class.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nExecuted repro (/tmp/repro_f1.py, output verbatim): a Release built from search-payload data ({id, title, year, resource_url} only) with a client whose _get raises discogs_client HTTPError 429 produced: degraded dict {album:'Kind Of Blue', year:'1959', label:None, catalog_number:None, cover_art_url:None, tracklist:[], genres:[]} with the only log line being 'Failed to fetch tracklist for release 123: 429'. Feeding that through MetadataResolver.resolve: 'resolver cached: source=MetadataSource.DISCOGS_COLLECTION, tracklist=[], cover=None' and for the album closer: 'is_last_track=False (gate on the Discogs Play Count write)'. Library mechanics confirmed from installed discogs_client/models.py: fetch() -> 'self.refresh()' -> 'data = self.client._get(self.data[resource_url])'.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
