  {
   "finding_id": "err-3",
   "title": "[R4:err-3] is_transient() classifies HTTP 401/403/404 as transient — a revoked Discogs token or wrong username silently stops play-count accrual forever, logged only as INFO \"(transient)\"",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `err-3` · **Wave 2 — Write-path integrity & the error taxonomy** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **MEDIUM**_\n\n**Location:** `src/metadata/errors.py:55`  \n**Lens:** Error handling, resilience & cross-fix interaction\n\n## Defect\nThe taxonomy's docstring defines Transient as 'network blip, timeout, 429, 5xx' (errors.py:7-8), but the implementation lists the whole requests.exceptions.RequestException family and discogs_client.exceptions.HTTPError with no status-code discrimination (errors.py:55-61). requests.HTTPError from raise_for_status() on a 401/403/404 — and discogs_client's HTTPError 401/404 — are definitive, permanent answers (revoked/invalid token, wrong username, deleted resource), yet is_transient() returns True for all of them (executed: all four cases -> True). Consequence for the explicitly-requested startup scenario 'config valid but Discogs token revoked': _get_collection_index's raise_for_status (reader.py:296) and the discogs_client search both raise 401s that the resolver logs as 'Discogs collection search couldn't determine (transient)' at INFO (resolver.py:137-138, 161-162) — permanently, for every track, forever. Every album resolves via FALLBACK (never cached, since discogs_completed=False), so no release_id is ever latched, and every completed record ends with the routine INFO 'release not in Discogs collection — skipping Play Count' (listen_tracker.py:342-346). The operator's collection silently stops accruing plays, and the only journal signal is an INFO line whose text actively asserts the opposite of the truth ('transient' — retry will fix it) about a condition that requires operator action. Nothing ever escalates to WARNING/ERROR no matter how long it persists. #149/META-6 added discogs_client.HTTPError to the transient list but did not subdivide by status; no closed issue covers permanent-status misclassification.\n\n## Failure scenario\nLane rotates or accidentally revokes the Discogs token (or typos discogs.username, producing 404s on the collection index). The app boots cleanly, Shazam recognition and the display keep working on fallback metadata (no year/label/catalog/tracklist), and every play for the following weeks is uncredited. The journal shows only INFO 'couldn't determine (transient)' lines — indistinguishable from a wifi blip — so the misconfiguration is discovered months later as a hole in the collection's play history.\n\n## Suggested fix\nIn is_transient(), inspect the status where available: for requests.HTTPError use exc.response.status_code, for discogs_client.exceptions.HTTPError use its status_code attribute; classify 429/408/5xx (and status-less connection errors/timeouts) as transient, and 4xx auth/permission/not-found as permanent. In the resolver, log permanent classifications at WARNING (or ERROR for 401/403 specifically, with 'check your token' text) so a dead credential is visibly distinct from a blip.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe suggested fix is sound; three refinements. (1) discogs_client.exceptions.HTTPError does carry status_code (verified: HTTPError('Unauthorized', 401).status_code == 401), so that branch works as written. (2) For requests.HTTPError, guard exc.response for None before reading status_code, and keep 429/408/5xx transient — the transport returns the 429 response after its single failed retry and the reader's raise_for_status then raises HTTPError(429), which MUST remain transient or a rate-limit spike would be mislogged as a permanent misconfig. (3) Keep the album uncached/retryable even for permanent-classified auth errors (discogs_completed=False semantics unchanged): the escalation should change the LOG level and text (\"check your Discogs token/username\"), not cache a downgrade — caching a FALLBACK on a 401 would pin the downgrade for the session after the operator fixes the token (re-introducing the B-4/#19 class). Consider throttling the WARNING/ERROR to avoid one per track (cf. #178's precedent for permanent-misconfig log throttling).\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nExecuted repro (/tmp/repro_f2.py): '401 via raise_for_status -> is_transient: True; 401 via discogs_client -> is_transient: True; 404 via discogs_client -> is_transient: True; 403 via raise_for_status -> is_transient: True'. Docstring contradiction: errors.py:7-8 defines Transient as 'network blip, timeout, 429, 5xx'; errors.py:55-61 lists the whole exception families unconditionally. Resolver logs the transient branch at INFO (resolver.py:138).\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
   "labels": [
    "code-review",
