  {
   "finding_id": "stab-2",
   "title": "[R4:stab-2] Collection index and album cache never expire within a process lifetime; the 'restarts daily' premise is unimplemented, so records added to Discogs during long uptimes are silently never Play-Count credited until a restart",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `stab-2` · **Wave 2 — Write-path integrity & the error taxonomy** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **MEDIUM**_\n\n**Location:** `src/metadata/discogs/reader.py:285`  \n**Lens:** Long-run stability & performance\n\n## Defect\nDiscogsReader._get_collection_index() caches the collection index forever (reader.py:285-286: `if self._collection_index is not None: return self._collection_index`) with the stated justification 'the collection is static within a session and the process restarts daily, so there is no TTL' (reader.py:263). MetadataResolver's album cache repeats the same premise ('the process restarts daily in practice', resolver.py:26). But nothing in the repo implements a daily restart: the documented systemd unit (docs/pi-setup-guide.md:354-355) is `Restart=on-failure` / `RestartSec=10` only — no timer, no RuntimeMaxSec, no OnCalendar anywhere (grep confirms) — and the product premise is an appliance that 'is on all the time' (docs/roadmap.md:393) running unattended 24/7. The prior rounds hardened this index in the opposite direction only: #169 declined persisting it ACROSS restarts; no finding examined its validity WITHIN an arbitrarily long uptime. On week-plus uptimes the index is a snapshot of the collection at first resolve after boot: any record added to Discogs afterwards fails strategy 1 (`index.get(release.id)` miss) AND strategy 2 (fuzzy over the same stale index), so search_collection returns None, the resolve completes cleanly via the DATABASE tier, the (artist, album) key is then cached as a DATABASE-tier result with no instance_id (resolver.py:156-158) — and every subsequent play of that record is silently never credited (no Play Count, no Last Played) until someone restarts the service. The B-4/B-13 retry machinery cannot help: this is a clean 'not owned' verdict, not a transient error, so it pins.\n\n## Failure scenario\nAppliance up for three weeks (normal: it only restarts on failure). Owner buys a record Saturday, adds it to their Discogs collection from their phone, plays it that evening — the closer plays, SESSION_ENDED fires, and _finalize_session logs 'release not in Discogs collection — skipping Play Count and Last Played updates (fallback metadata)' (or credits nothing via the DATABASE tier). Every play of every newly-added record is silently uncounted until the next reboot/crash, with nothing in the journal that looks like an error.\n\n## Suggested fix\nGive the collection index a TTL (e.g. 12-24h: on access, rebuild if older — one paginated re-fetch of ~1 request per 100 releases, trivially within the 60/min limit) or, more surgically, refresh it at most once per cooldown when strategy 1 finds a DATABASE-confirmed release that is absent from the index (the exact signature of a stale index). Also drop the two 'restarts daily' docstring claims, or actually implement the restart (systemd RuntimeMaxSec / a timer) — either makes the premise true; today neither side does. Keep #169's constraint: the refresh stays in-memory-from-API, never a persisted index.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe suggested fix is sound but incomplete on one point: a TTL (or targeted refresh) on the READER's index alone does not un-pin albums the RESOLVER has already cached as DATABASE-tier — resolver.py's _album_cache also has no TTL, so an album played once before the refresh stays downgraded until it churns out of the 64-entry LRU (which may be never in a session that touches few albums). A complete fix must pair the index refresh with resolver-cache invalidation for DATABASE/FALLBACK-tier entries (or give that cache the same TTL). Second refinement: the 'refresh when strategy 1 finds a DATABASE-confirmed release absent from the index' trigger fires on EVERY genuinely-unowned album too (the common case for database-tier resolves), so the cooldown the finding mentions is load-bearing, not optional — without it each unowned-album resolve costs a full collection re-page. Third: whichever way it goes, fix all THREE stale docstrings — the finding cites reader.py:263 and resolver.py:26 but misses cover_cache.py:328 ('daily-restart cadence'), which leans on the same unimplemented premise.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nreader.py:285-286: `if self._collection_index is not None:\\n            return self._collection_index` — assigned only in __init__ (None) and at build; grep shows no invalidation site. reader.py:262-263: 'The collection is static within a session and the process restarts daily, so there is no TTL.' resolver.py:25-26: 'deliberately has no TTL ... the process restarts daily in practice.' docs/pi-setup-guide.md:354-355: `Restart=on-failure` / `RestartSec=10`; grep -rn 'RuntimeMaxSec|OnCalendar|timer|daily restart' across scripts/ and docs/ returns nothing implementing a periodic restart. Miss path: reader.py:127-134 (strategy 1 index.get) and 146-159 (strategy 2 iterates the same stale index); resolver.py:156-158 then caches the DATABASE-tier downgrade because both tiers 'completed cleanly'.\n\n</details>\n\n---\nNote from triage: reconcile with the **#169** deferral decision (disk-TTL collection cache, closed as documented deferral). The legitimate fix here may be one line of systemd timer config implementing the 'restarts daily' premise rather than an in-process TTL; the finding stands because today NEITHER exists.\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
   "labels": [
    "code-review",
    "bug",
    "severity:medium"
