  },
  {
   "finding_id": "err-2",
   "title": "[R4:err-2] Stale shared-pool comments + false \"caller has no further retry\" log; #163's finalize retry burns all 3 attempts inside a >10s Retry-After window (loss itself already accepted in #88)",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `err-2` · **Wave 2 — Write-path integrity & the error taxonomy** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **LOW**_\n\n**Location:** `src/metadata/discogs/transport.py:180`  \n**Lens:** Error handling, resilience & cross-fix interaction\n\n## Defect\nTwo prior fixes compose incoherently on the Play Count credit path. META-10 (#88) made the transport skip its 429 retry when Retry-After exceeds _RATE_LIMIT_MAX_WAIT=10s, 'so the futile retry is skipped... no second request hammering Discogs mid-backoff' (transport.py:170-187), logging 'its caller has no further retry, so the write may be lost'. But #163 later gave the caller exactly that retry: _finalize_write_with_retry (listen_tracker.py:388-417) re-runs increment_play_count 3 times with 1s+2s backoff — so when Discogs asks for a 60s wait, all three POSTs land inside the same throttle window within 3.0 seconds (guaranteed 429s, the precise hammering META-10's comment says it avoids), the credit is lost anyway, and the journal carries three copies of a factually wrong 'caller has no further retry' ERROR. Meanwhile the cap's entire justification is a stale premise: transport.py:36-40 still says 'request() runs on the SHARED run_in_executor(None,...) pool, which also serves cover downloads and Last.fm scrobbles' and lines 42-45/177-179 defer waiting out a long Retry-After 'to the dedicated executor (#61)' — but #61 SHIPPED: the dedicated 2-worker Discogs pool is constructed 80 lines below in this same file (transport.py:121), and every credit write runs on it (writer.run -> DiscogsHttp.run). At session end nothing else needs that pool, so honoring a 60s Retry-After there would cost nothing the cap was protecting; the deferral's precondition was satisfied and the behavior never revisited.\n\n## Failure scenario\nA record finishes right after a burst of Discogs traffic (fresh collection-index build + searches for a new album). The closing credit's POST gets 429 with Retry-After: 60. Transport declines to wait; the finalize layer retries at +1s and +3s — both still throttled — and after 3.0s total the completed play's Play Count credit is permanently lost ('credit LOST... nothing will retry it'), despite the app having a dedicated idle thread pool that could simply have slept out the 60s and landed the write.\n\n## Suggested fix\nNow that Discogs calls run on their own pool, raise _RATE_LIMIT_MAX_WAIT for writes (e.g. honor Retry-After up to ~90s on the dedicated executor, keeping the drain-window interplay in mind), or thread the Retry-After value up so _finalize_write_with_retry uses it as the backoff instead of 1s/2s. At minimum, fix the two stale comments (transport.py:36-45, 177-179) and the 'caller has no further retry' log text, which is wrong for both finalize-retried writes.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nPrefer threading Retry-After up over raising _RATE_LIMIT_MAX_WAIT. Raising the cap to ~90s has costs the finding underweights: (a) transport.close() (transport.py:223-231) reasons \"bounded by its 15s socket timeout plus at most one 10s backoff sleep\" — a 60-90s time.sleep() in a worker delays interpreter exit by up to that long even with wait=False, because concurrent.futures joins non-daemon workers at exit; (b) _SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_SECONDS is 10.0, so a 60s in-thread sleep is abandoned at shutdown anyway; (c) on the album-split path _finalize_write_with_retry runs under the lifecycle lock, so a 60s backoff there stalls new-session handling for a minute. Threading the Retry-After value up to _finalize_write_with_retry and awaiting it via asyncio.sleep in the event loop avoids all three (cancellable, no parked thread), with the honored wait capped and the lock interplay decided explicitly. The minimum, non-negotiable fix is cheap and behavior-free: rewrite transport.py:36-45 and 173-179 to the post-#61 reality, and correct the two ERROR strings — noting the \"no further retry\" text is accurate for update_last_played but false for the increment, so the message should either name the distinction or drop the claim.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nExecuted repro (/tmp/repro_f6.py): with session.post stubbed to return 429 + Retry-After: 60, ListenTracker._finalize_write_with_retry over writer.increment_play_count produced: 3x 'server asked to wait 60s, beyond our 10s cap — NOT retrying... its caller has no further retry' ERRORs, then 'credit landed: False; POSTs issued: 3; elapsed 3.0s vs server-requested 60s window; POST spacing: [1.00s, 2.00s]'. Stale-premise evidence: transport.py:37 'request() runs on the SHARED run_in_executor(None,…) pool' vs transport.py:111-121 '#61: Discogs blocking calls... run on this DEDICATED pool, never the shared default'.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
   "labels": [
    "code-review",
    "bug",
    "severity:low"
   ],
   "milestone_title": "Wave 2 — Write-path integrity & the error taxonomy"
  },
  {
   "finding_id": "stab-1",
   "title": "[R4:stab-1] No music gate on recognition: every silent chunk is Shazam-POSTed (~8,640/day idle), despite the silence verdict being computed one line earlier",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `stab-1` · **Wave 3 — Recognition & session lifecycle on real hardware** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **HIGH**_\n\n**Location:** `src/audio/capture.py:365`  \n**Lens:** Long-run stability & performance\n\n## Defect\nAudioCapture.run() enqueues EVERY assembled chunk for recognition unconditionally (capture.py:361-365: `self.silence.process(chunk, ...)` then `await self.recognizer.enqueue(chunk, ...)`), and RecognitionLoop.run() calls `backend.recognize()` on every dequeued chunk with no music/silence gate. shazamio 0.8.1's Rust core generates a valid signature even for digitally silent audio (executed probe: `recognize_bytes` on 15s of zeros returns a Signature in ~0.1s, no exception), and `Shazam.send_recognize_request_v2` then unconditionally POSTs it to Shazam's private API. So on this 24/7 appliance, with the default 15s chunk / 5s overlap (10s hop), a silent house generates one HTTPS POST to the unofficial Shazam endpoint plus one 1.3MB WAV encode (measured 4.5ms on x86; several times that on a Pi 4) plus one Rust DSP pass every 10 seconds, forever — ~8,640 requests/day, ~3.15M/year, the overwhelming majority of all traffic for a turntable played a few hours a day. The app already computes the authoritative music/silence classification for the SAME chunk one line earlier (SilenceDetector.process, with SIL-4 hysteresis; `is_music_playing` is a public property with zero production consumers — grep confirms only tests read it). None of the 178 closed issues covers this: P-6/PCONC-2 bounded the per-call cost, never the call count. Beyond pure waste (CPU, TLS handshakes, power), the entire identification capability of the appliance depends on the tolerance of an unofficial API; sustained fixed-cadence 24/7 polling from one residential IP is exactly the traffic profile that gets throttled or blocked, which would break recognition when music actually plays.\n\n## Failure scenario\nTurntable idle overnight and the owner at work: 20h of silence -> ~7,200 pointless Shazam POSTs that day, every day. Over weeks of uptime this is millions of requests for zero product value; if Shazam rate-limits or blocks the IP (their prerogative against an unofficial client), the next record played latches the display to NO MATCH FOUND — core-feature loss caused entirely by traffic generated while the app itself had classified the room as silent (verified in repro: chunks reach backend.recognize() with is_music_playing=False and status=IDLE).\n\n## Suggested fix\nGate the enqueue on the silence detector the capture loop already consults for the same chunk: `self.silence.process(chunk, sr); if self.silence.is_music_playing: await self.recognizer.enqueue(chunk, sr)`. The ordering is already correct — process() runs first, so the chunk that trips MUSIC_STARTED reports is_music_playing=True and is still recognized (no missed session start), and the SIL-4 hysteresis dead band keeps quiet passages mid-record classified as music. Optionally also drain/ignore stale queued chunks whose epoch predates the silence transition.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe suggested gate (`if self.silence.is_music_playing: await self.recognizer.enqueue(...)`) is correctly ordered — process() runs first, so the chunk that trips MUSIC_STARTED is enqueued, and the SIL-4 dead band keeps quiet passages mid-record classified as music; the ERROR-recovery path (reposition needle → MUSIC_STARTED from ERROR, main.py:187-189) also still works since that same chunk passes the gate. Two corrections/additions: (a) NEW COUPLING, worth stating in the issue: today a mis-calibrated silence_threshold_rms (too high for a quiet pressing/preamp level) degrades only the session lifecycle — recognition still identifies and displays the track; after the gate, that misconfiguration also silences recognition entirely. Mitigate by logging suppressed chunks at debug/info (a \"capture live but recognition gated\" breadcrumb for the journal), and consider gating on the EXIT threshold semantics the detector already embodies rather than adding a second RMS check. (b) The optional \"drain stale queued chunks by epoch\" is mostly unnecessary: the queue is maxsize=5 and stops being fed once gated, so at most ~5 trailing recognitions (~50s) run after music stops, and the PCONC-1 epoch guard already prevents them committing across a session boundary — I'd drop that half to keep the fix one line plus a log. Also note tests to update: any capture-loop test asserting enqueue-per-chunk (TQ-7/#138 added coverage for run()) plus a new RED-first test that silent chunks do NOT reach the backend and the MUSIC_STARTED chunk DOES.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\ncapture.py:361-365: `for chunk in assembler.feed(block): self.silence.process(chunk, self.sample_rate); await self.recognizer.enqueue(chunk, self.sample_rate)` — no gate. recognizer.py:361-364 calls `self.backend.recognize(audio, sample_rate)` for every dequeued chunk. Executed probe 1: shazamio_core Recognizer on 15s of zeros -> 'signature OK in 0.101s' and on sub-threshold noise -> 'signature OK in 0.083s' (no exception, so the network POST in send_recognize_request_v2 — source inspected, unconditional http_client.request('POST', ...) — always fires). Executed probe 2 (full pipeline sim, ChunkAssembler+SilenceDetector+RecognitionLoop with counting backend, 120s of zeros): 'is_music_playing=False status=IDLE ... backend.recognize() (network) calls: 5' (only 5 of 11 because the sim feeds faster than real time and drop-oldest engages; at the real 10s cadence every chunk is recognized) '=> at the default 10s hop that is 8640 Shazam POSTs per silent day'. WAV encode measured: 4.5ms / 1,323,044 bytes per 15s chunk. grep is_music_playing: only src/audio/silence.py:188 (definition) and tests — no production consumer.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n⚠ **Ship together with the conc-3 issue.** The natural fix — gate recognition on `silence.is_music_playing` — composes badly with conc-3: under a mis-set capture gain the detector never reports music, so a naive gate turns 'session never ends' into 'recognition never runs at all'. The gate needs conc-3's detector-feedback path (commit-while-silent arms the detector / loud calibration warning) shipped with or before it.\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
