  {
   "finding_id": "conc-1",
   "title": "[R4:conc-1] Rebuild-loop device re-resolution reads PortAudio's import-time snapshot: comment and #164 test promise late-enumeration/re-plug recovery the library cannot deliver; a device absent at import or re-plugged to a new card index leaves ca…",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `conc-1` · **Wave 3 — Recognition & session lifecycle on real hardware** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (verified by code reading) · severity **MEDIUM**_\n\n**Location:** `src/audio/capture.py:150`  \n**Lens:** Concurrency & asyncio lifecycle\n\n## Defect\nThe #164 fix moved _find_device_index() inside the rebuild/retry loop, and its comment (capture.py:321-330) claims a device 'not yet enumerated when the service starts' degrades to backoff-and-rebuild, and that 're-resolving each attempt also picks up a device that reappears on a different index after a re-plug'. Neither claim can hold: sounddevice calls Pa_Initialize() once at `import sounddevice` (sounddevice.py:2972 `_initialize()`), and query_devices() merely iterates `Pa_GetDeviceCount()` over PortAudio's device table, which is frozen at initialization — PortAudio never rescans. The repo never calls sd._terminate()/sd._initialize() (grep confirms only capture.py:150 touches the device API), so every retry sees the identical stale snapshot. The unit test that pins this recovery (tests/test_capture.py:575, test_absent_device_at_startup_is_retried_not_crash_looped) only passes because its query_devices MOCK returns a different list on the second call — exactly what the real library cannot do. Worse, #164 removed the accidental recovery path: pre-#164 the ValueError escaped run(), systemd restarted the process, and the fresh Pa_Initialize saw the now-enumerated device (bounded by StartLimitBurst). Post-#164 the process retries forever inside run() with a #178-throttled error line, so systemd never restarts it and capture is dead until a human intervenes. CRIT-4/#83 already established the unit orders only on network.target, so 'USB slower than service start' is the named realistic boot condition.\n\n## Failure scenario\nPi boots; systemd starts vinyl-now-playing before the UCA222 finishes USB enumeration; `import sounddevice` runs Pa_Initialize against a device table without the interface. _find_device_index() raises 'not found' on attempt 1 and on every 1s retry forever — the frozen table can never contain the device. The process stays alive (no crash loop, no systemd Restart), logs one throttled error per 30s, and the display sits on IDLE indefinitely while records play unrecognized. Same for a mid-run unplug→replug to a different ALSA card index after a CONC-5 stall: the rebuilt lookup returns the stale index, sd.InputStream(device=stale) raises every retry, and capture never comes back.\n\n## Suggested fix\nRefresh the PortAudio device table before each failed-lookup retry (no stream is open at that point in the rebuild loop): on lookup/open failure call sd._terminate(); sd._initialize() (the documented python-sounddevice rescan recipe) before the next _find_device_index(); alternatively, after N consecutive lookup failures re-raise out of run() so systemd's bounded Restart provides a fresh Pa_Initialize. Fix the test to model the real constraint (assert the refresh happens) instead of a mock that silently re-enumerates.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe _terminate()+_initialize() refresh is the correct documented recipe, with three corrections: (1) invoke it only on the failure path (after a failed _find_device_index() or InputStream construction/stall teardown), never unconditionally per rebuild, and never while any stream object exists — at capture.py:331 the previous stream has exited its context manager, so this holds, but guard/comment it since _terminate() with a live stream is unsafe; (2) both are private APIs — pin/note the sounddevice version and wrap in try/except so a refactor upstream degrades to the current behavior rather than crashing the loop; (3) _initialize() re-enumerates ALSA synchronously (up to a few hundred ms) on the event loop — acceptable at the 1s backoff cadence but worth an explicit comment, or run_in_executor if display smoothness matters during retry. Also fix the capture.py:321-330 comment to state the real semantics, and rewrite test_absent_device_at_startup_is_retried_not_crash_looped to assert the refresh call happens between failed lookups instead of a mock that silently re-enumerates. The alternative fix (re-raise after N failures to let systemd restart) interacts badly with StartLimitBurst=5/300s — repeated escalations would permanently fail the unit and re-trigger the STAB-4 Discogs re-index hammering the unit config exists to prevent; prefer the in-process refresh.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\ncapture.py:150 `devices = sd.query_devices()`; capture.py:328-330 comment: 're-resolving each attempt also picks up a device that reappears on a different index after a re-plug'. Installed sounddevice 0.5.5: line 2972 `_initialize()` executed at import (`_check(_lib.Pa_Initialize(), ...)`), query_devices → `for i in range(_check(_lib.Pa_GetDeviceCount()))` over the static table; no rescan API exists in the module besides _terminate/_initialize, and `grep -rn '_terminate\\|_initialize' src/ main.py` finds no caller. tests/test_capture.py:591-596 makes the recovery green only by mocking query_devices to return `[device('USB Audio Codec', 2)]` on call 2 after `[device('Some Output-Only Sink', 0)]` on call 1.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
   "labels": [
    "code-review",
    "bug",
    "severity:medium"
