  },
  {
   "finding_id": "conc-3",
   "title": "[R4:conc-3] Recognition commits are not gated on the silence detector's music state: sub-threshold audio yields sessions SESSION_ENDED can never end — overnight stuck card, wrong/merged Discogs credits (album-split partially recovers), lost loves;…",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `conc-3` · **Wave 3 — Recognition & session lifecycle on real hardware** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **MEDIUM**_\n\n**Location:** `src/audio/silence.py:133`  \n**Lens:** Concurrency & asyncio lifecycle\n\n## Defect\ncapture.run feeds EVERY chunk to the recognizer unconditionally (capture.py:361-365), and TrackCommitService.commit gates only on the session epoch — never on whether the SilenceDetector ever saw music. Meanwhile the whole session-teardown machinery (state.clear(), the B-1 epoch bump, the tracker's SESSION_ENDED credit) hangs exclusively off SESSION_ENDED, and _check_session_end requires `_silence_since is not None` — which is armed ONLY by a music→silence transition in process() (silence.py:108-123) or by reset_music_state(was_music=True). If the capture gain is low enough that music RMS sits below silence_threshold_rms (a mis-set alsamixer / preamp — a classic first-boot hardware condition), Shazam still confirms tracks (recognition works at absolute levels far below any RMS gate): commit() runs, set_track shows the card, on_track_identified starts a tracker session — but the detector never leaves silence-state, so _silence_since is never armed, MUSIC_STOPPED never fires, and when the record ends neither process() nor the wall-clock tick() can EVER emit SESSION_ENDED. The card stays on screen forever, the session epoch never bumps, the session is immortal, and the album's Play Count / Last Played / love are silently never written — while the appliance LOOKS like it works (cards appear on every track), making the miscalibration invisible. The same latch applies after any normal session end mid-operation (_session_ended stays True until a MUSIC_STARTED that sub-threshold audio can never produce). Every prior silence-lens fix (SIL-1/2/4, B-6 ticker) hardened the timer's arithmetic; none noticed that the recognition pipeline can drive the entire commit path while the detector believes no music has ever played.\n\n## Failure scenario\nOperator wires the turntable through a line-in with the capture level at 20%: chunk RMS ≈ 0.005 vs silence_threshold_rms 0.01. Executed probe (/tmp/probe_quiet_session.py, real SilenceDetector): 10 simulated minutes of sub-threshold music then an hour of dead silence with tick() running → events emitted: [] both times, _silence_since stays None, _session_ended False. In the app those same chunks flow to RecognitionLoop (capture.py:365), tracks confirm and commit (epoch 0 == epoch 0 passes), the museum card shows every track — and when the record ends the card stays up all night, the tracker session never finalizes, and Discogs/Last.fm are never updated. Recovery requires a process restart or a gain fix; nothing in the journal explains why.\n\n## Suggested fix\nMake commit-time recognition evidence reach the detector: when a track commits while `silence.is_music_playing` is False, log a loud calibration warning ('track confirmed while the silence detector reads silence — check capture gain / silence_threshold_rms') and give SilenceDetector a `note_music_evidence()` that arms the session-end machinery (e.g. treat the commit as music, entering the music state with an exit threshold derived from recently observed RMS rather than the configured one, so the eventual true silence still lands SESSION_ENDED). At minimum, the warning turns an invisible permanent degradation into a diagnosable first-boot line.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe minimal fix (a loud calibration warning when commit() runs while silence.is_music_playing is False) is correct, cheap, and should be the accepted scope — it turns an invisible miscalibration into a first-boot diagnosable log line. The fuller note_music_evidence() auto-arm is riskier than the finding implies: synthesizing a music state from recognition evidence with an exit threshold \"derived from recently observed RMS\" makes the classifier state depend on network recognition results and observed-gain heuristics, and a wrong derived exit threshold could flap or end sessions early — exactly the class SIL-4 hysteresis was built to prevent. If auto-arming is pursued, prefer the simplest safe variant: on commit-while-silent, enter music state and recompute exit_threshold from the max chunk RMS seen since the last commit (times _MUSIC_EXIT_RATIO), and log that the thresholds were auto-lowered. The fix description should also drop the claim that Last.fm is never updated (scrobbles work; only the love is lost) and note the album-split recovery, so the filed issue does not overstate the blast radius.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nsilence.py:126-133: the silence branch only ever calls `_check_session_end(now)`; `_silence_since` is assigned solely at silence.py:122 (music→silence transition) and silence.py:185 (reset with was_music). silence.py:141-145: `_check_session_end` requires `self._silence_since is not None`. capture.py:363-365: `self.silence.process(chunk, ...)` then unconditional `await self.recognizer.enqueue(chunk, ...)`. track_commit_service.py:104-113: commit checks only `self.state.session_epoch != audio_epoch` before set_track. Probe output: 'events emitted over 10 simulated minutes of sub-threshold music: []' and 'events after the record ended + 1h of dead silence: []', `_silence_since: None`.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n⚠ **Ship together with the stab-1 issue.** stab-1's silence gate depends on this finding's feedback path being in place first — see the stab-1 issue body for the interaction.\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
   "labels": [
    "code-review",
    "bug",
    "severity:medium"
