  },
  {
   "finding_id": "conc-4",
   "title": "[R4:conc-4] commit() logs 'Now playing' and returns True after the tracker's CONC-6 stale drop, contradicting its documented False-on-discard contract",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `conc-4` · **Wave 3 — Recognition & session lifecycle on real hardware** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **LOW**_\n\n**Location:** `src/app/track_commit_service.py:140`  \n**Lens:** Concurrency & asyncio lifecycle\n\n## Defect\ncommit()'s docstring promises: 'Returns False when the commit was discarded because the session that produced this audio has ended' (track_commit_service.py:80-83). The post-resolve discard path honors that (returns False, line 111). But when the session ends while on_track_identified waits on the lifecycle lock, the tracker drops the track via is_stale (listen_tracker.py:541-548) and commit resumes: the epoch guards correctly skip set_raw and the scrobble, yet execution falls through the unconditional `log.info('Now playing: ...')` at line 140-143 and reaches `return True` at line 174. So a discarded stale commit logs a now-playing line for a track that was already cleared off the screen by clear(), and reports success. RecognitionLoop currently ignores the return value ('the loop awaits it but doesn't inspect the result'), so today the damage is a misleading journal entry during exactly the race windows the operator would be debugging — but any future caller that starts trusting the documented boolean (e.g. to decide whether to clear the pending candidate) inherits a wrong answer on precisely the stale path the contract exists to flag.\n\n## Failure scenario\nNeedle lifts; SESSION_ENDED's clear() lands while commit is parked on the tracker's lifecycle lock behind a detach. The tracker logs 'Dropping stale track ... (CONC-6)', state is IDLE, the card is gone — and the very next journal lines are 'Now playing: Artist / Album / Title' and commit returns True. An operator (or test) correlating the journal with the display sees a now-playing claim for a track that is not playing and was never tracked or scrobbled.\n\n## Suggested fix\nAfter `await self.tracker.on_track_identified(...)`, re-check the epoch once and take the same discard exit as the post-resolve path: `if self.state.session_epoch != audio_epoch: log.info('Discarding stale commit ...'); return False` — placing the 'Now playing' log and the scrobble behind it (the existing set_raw/scrobble guards then collapse into that single check).\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe suggested fix (single epoch re-check after on_track_identified; place the \"Now playing\" log, set_raw, and scrobble behind it; return False on mismatch) is sound and collapses the two existing guards into one. One caveat: an epoch mismatch at that point conflates two sub-cases — (a) the tracker dropped the track stale under the lock (nothing recorded), and (b) the tracker ACCEPTED the track and the session ended during its album-split Discogs write (a play may already be counted). Returning False and suppressing the log is right in both, but the discard log message should not claim nothing happened; either differentiate the message or note in a comment that in sub-case (b) the tracker may have recorded the play before the session ended. Also keep the B-19/STAB-2 clock-sanity gate inside the new guarded block unchanged.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\ntrack_commit_service.py:138-143: `if self.state.session_epoch == audio_epoch: self.state.set_raw(raw)` immediately followed by the UNguarded `log.info(f\"Now playing: {metadata.artist} / ...\")`; line 174 `return True` is the only terminal return after that point. Docstring contract at lines 80-83. The CONC-6 drop that makes this reachable: listen_tracker.py:541-548 returns without logging the track, while the caller proceeds to the success log and True.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
   "labels": [
    "code-review",
    "bug",
    "severity:low"
   ],
   "milestone_title": "Wave 3 — Recognition & session lifecycle on real hardware"
  },
  {
   "finding_id": "err-5",
   "title": "[R4:err-5] Per-chunk 'ShazamIO recognition failed' WARNING is unthrottled — a fast-failing network outage writes ~8,640 identical journal lines/day, the flood class #178/PCONC-4 throttled in capture.py",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `err-5` · **Wave 3 — Recognition & session lifecycle on real hardware** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **LOW**_\n\n**Location:** `src/audio/recognizer.py:106`  \n**Lens:** Error handling, resilience & cross-fix interaction\n\n## Defect\nThe repo has an established policy that repeating failure logs on an unattended SD-card appliance must be throttled: #178 throttled the capture-loop error (capture.py:76-82, _CAPTURE_ERROR_WARN_INTERVAL_SECONDS=30), and PCONC-4 throttled block-drop warnings (capture.py:57-62). The recognition failure path never got the same treatment: ShazamIOBackend.recognize's broad except logs 'ShazamIO recognition failed: {e}' at WARNING once per chunk (recognizer.py:105-107). Chunks are emitted every chunk_seconds - overlap_seconds = 10s (config.example.yaml: 15/5) and — because AudioCapture.run() feeds EVERY assembled chunk to recognizer.enqueue() unconditionally, silence included (capture.py:361-365), and RecognitionLoop.run() recognizes every dequeued chunk with no music/status gate — the cadence is 24/7, not just while music plays. During a sustained network outage (router down, ISP outage, wifi degradation — the answer to 'what does a week of network down produce'), that is ~6 WARNINGs/min, ~8,640/day, ~60k/week of identical lines, each also preceded by a fruitless executor WAV-encode. The capture leg, by contrast, would summarize the same week in ~20k periodic lines only if the stream itself failed; here the stream is healthy and only the network is down, so this is the dominant flood.\n\n## Failure scenario\nHome internet goes down Friday evening of a holiday weekend; the Pi keeps capturing (a healthy silent stream), Shazam connect attempts fail fast, and by Monday the journal holds ~26,000 near-identical WARNING lines drowning the few lines that matter (session events, the one-off degradations from other components), with corresponding journald write amplification on the SD card the project's own #178/PCONC-4/STAB-6 work exists to avoid.\n\n## Suggested fix\nApply the #178 pattern to the recognize failure path: count identical consecutive failures and log the first immediately, then a summarizing WARNING at most every ~60s ('recognition failing: N attempts since last report'), resetting on success or message change. (Optionally, and separately, consider gating recognition on the silence detector's music state so silent chunks skip the Shazam call entirely — that would also cut ~8.6k needless daily requests to an unofficial API — but that is a design change beyond this lens.)\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe suggested fix (count identical consecutive failures, first logs immediately, summarize at most every ~60s, reset on success or message change — the #178 pattern) is right, but it covers only the fast-fail leg. An outage that HANGS instead of failing fast (black-holed packets rather than connection-refused/DNS) is cancelled by run()'s 30s wait_for (recognizer.py:361-364) BEFORE recognize's except can log, and lands in run()'s own unthrottled log.error('Recognition loop error: …') at recognizer.py:374 — ~1 ERROR per ~32s (30s timeout + 2s sleep), ~2,700 lines/day, same class. A complete fix throttles both legs (e.g. a small shared same-message/interval throttled-log helper used at both sites), otherwise the outage mode determines whether the flood merely moves from WARNING to ERROR. Also note journald's default rate limiting (10000 msgs/30s) does not mitigate either leg — 6/min is far below it — so there is no accidental backstop.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nrecognizer.py:105-107: 'except Exception as e: log.warning(f\"ShazamIO recognition failed: {e}\")' — no counter, no interval, in contrast to capture.py:241-267 (_log_capture_error, #178) and capture.py:193-215 (PCONC-4 drop throttle) in the same pipeline. Unconditional enqueue: capture.py:361-365 ('for chunk in assembler.feed(block): self.silence.process(...); await self.recognizer.enqueue(...)'). Cadence: config.example.yaml chunk_seconds: 15, overlap_seconds: 5 -> one chunk per 10s.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
   "labels": [
    "code-review",
    "bug",
    "severity:low"
   ],
   "milestone_title": "Wave 3 — Recognition & session lifecycle on real hardware"
  },
  {
   "finding_id": "ops-1",
   "title": "[R4:ops-1] Fresh install per setup guide is dead: Imager's default \"Raspberry Pi OS (64-bit)\" is now Trixie/Python 3.13, where shazamio's pydub dep cannot import (audioop removed) — pip succeeds, every recognition chunk fails silently as a miss",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `ops-1` · **Wave 4 — First boot: install path, operator tooling & token hygiene** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **HIGH**_\n\n**Location:** `requirements.txt:1`  \n**Lens:** Operability, config & Pi bring-up\n\n## Defect\nrequirements.txt:1 pins the world to \"Python 3.11 (Raspberry Pi OS bookworm system Python)\" and docs/pi-setup-guide.md:13 tells the operator to flash \"Raspberry Pi OS (64-bit) — the full Desktop version\" via Raspberry Pi Imager. That Imager entry no longer resolves to Bookworm: the live os_list feed shows \"Raspberry Pi OS (64-bit)\" = 2026-06-18-raspios-TRIXIE-arm64.img.xz (Bookworm survives only as \"Raspberry Pi OS (Legacy, 64-bit)\" buried under \"other\"). Trixie ships Python 3.13, where the audioop stdlib module was removed (PEP 594). `pip install -r requirements.txt` on 3.13 SUCCEEDS silently (shazamio-core 1.1.2 publishes cp313 manylinux_2_28 aarch64 wheels; pydub is pure Python; nothing declares requires-python or audioop-lts), but `from shazamio import Shazam` fails at runtime: shazamio>=0.6.0 resolves to 0.8.1, whose api.py line 8 does `from pydub import AudioSegment`, and pydub 0.25.1 (the only version shazamio accepts, unmaintained since 2021) imports audioop/pyaudioop at module import. The failure surfaces in the worst possible place: the shazamio import is deliberately lazy inside ShazamIOBackend._call_shazam (src/audio/recognizer.py:115), which sits under recognize()'s broad except (recognizer.py:105-107) — so the app boots cleanly, the display comes up, capture runs, and every recognition chunk logs `ShazamIO recognition failed: No module named 'pyaudioop'` as a WARNING and counts as a miss. After error_after_misses the display latches to NO MATCH FOUND for every record, forever. No startup check ever detects it and systemd sees a healthy service.\n\n## Failure scenario\nOperator follows pi-setup-guide.md step 1 verbatim today: Imager → \"Raspberry Pi OS (64-bit)\" → Trixie flashed → python3 -m venv creates a 3.13 venv → pip install -r requirements.txt completes green → every pre-flight check in the guide passes (Discogs live check, sounddevice device check, pygame display) → first needle drop: display shows LISTENING, then \"NO MATCH FOUND\"; journal fills with one 'ShazamIO recognition failed: No module named pyaudioop' warning per ~10s chunk. Nothing in the guide, the first-boot checklist, or the app's startup path connects that message to the Python version, and the appliance is 100% non-functional.\n\n## Suggested fix\nThree layers: (1) docs/pi-setup-guide.md step 1 must name the exact image — \"Raspberry Pi OS (Legacy, 64-bit)\" / Bookworm — or drop the Bookworm assumption; (2) requirements.txt: add `audioop-lts>=0.2; python_version >= \"3.13\"` (restores the audioop module pydub needs) so the current default OS works too; (3) fail loudly at startup instead of per-chunk: in build_components (or a RecognitionConfig-time probe when backend == \"shazamio\"), do the `from shazamio import Shazam` import eagerly and turn ImportError into an actionable ConfigError/log line naming Python 3.13/audioop — the lazy-import-for-testability seam can stay by catching only in test contexts (sounddevice is already stubbed by conftest, and the import is cheap).\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe three-layer fix is sound; refinements: (1) In the guide, name the exact Imager path — \"Raspberry Pi OS (other) → Raspberry Pi OS (Legacy, 64-bit)\" (it is nested, not top-level) — AND add a `python3 --version` expected-output check (3.11.x for Bookworm) right before the venv step, per the guide's existing expected-output style. (2) `audioop-lts>=0.2; python_version >= \"3.13\"` is the correct marker syntax and works because pydub tries `import audioop` before its `pyaudioop` fallback and audioop-lts installs the `audioop` module; note this makes 3.13 actually work rather than just documented-against, which is the more durable layer since the Legacy image will eventually disappear too. (3) For the fail-loud layer, prefer a one-time eager probe in build_components gated on backend == \"shazamio\" (turning ImportError into the existing ConfigError path with a message naming Python 3.13/audioop/audioop-lts) rather than removing the lazy import from recognizer.py — the A-13/#17 resolution deliberately kept that seam and the suite depends on the module importing without the audio stack; a probe at composition time preserves it while moving the failure from a per-chunk WARNING to a startup error systemd will surface.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nExecuted: curl of downloads.raspberrypi.org/os_list_imagingutility_v4.json → `Raspberry Pi OS (64-bit) | 2026-06-18 | ...raspios-trixie-arm64.img.xz` (Bookworm only under \"Legacy\"). PyPI: shazamio latest 0.8.1 requires `pydub<0.26.0,>=0.25.1`, `shazamio-core==1.1.2`; shazamio-core wheel list includes cp313 manylinux_2_28_aarch64 (installs clean on Trixie). Wheel inspection: shazamio/api.py:8 `from pydub import AudioSegment` (also converter.py:3, utils.py:12); shazamio/__init__.py `from .api import Shazam`. pydub/utils.py:14-16 `import audioop` / fallback `import pyaudioop`. Executed simulation of audioop-less Python: `import pydub` → `pydub import FAILED: ModuleNotFoundError No module named 'pyaudioop'`. Sink: src/audio/recognizer.py:105-107 `except Exception as e: log.warning(f\"ShazamIO recognition failed: {e}\"); return None` around the lazy `from shazamio import Shazam, HTTPClient` at recognizer.py:115. requirements.txt declares no audioop-lts and no python-version guard.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
