  {
   "finding_id": "ops-3",
   "title": "[R4:ops-3] Doc's only remedy for the boot-time session race (After=graphical-session.target in a system unit) is a verified silent no-op; StartLimitBurst=5/RestartSec=10 lets the race exhaust retries in ~50s",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `ops-3` · **Wave 4 — First boot: install path, operator tooling & token hygiene** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **MEDIUM**_\n\n**Location:** `docs/pi-setup-guide.md:561`  \n**Lens:** Operability, config & Pi bring-up\n\n## Defect\nThe Troubleshooting entry \"App starts but pygame window is invisible\" (pi-setup-guide.md:559-561) diagnoses the real first-boot race — the system service starting before the user's graphical session is up and accepting connections on DISPLAY=:0 — and prescribes `Add After=graphical-session.target to [Unit]`. Per systemd.special(7), graphical-session.target exists only in per-user service managers; in the system manager the unit does not exist, and systemd silently ignores ordering dependencies on nonexistent units. The edit changes nothing, and the race it addresses is the plausible one: the unit is `WantedBy=graphical.target` / `After=graphical.target` (line 343/359), but graphical.target is reached when the display manager *starts*, not when the autologin session (and Xwayland socket) is up. The backstop is Restart=on-failure every 10s — but StartLimitIntervalSec=300/StartLimitBurst=5 (line 344-345, added for STAB-4) means five failed connect attempts inside ~50 seconds permanently fail the unit; a Pi 4 desktop that takes just under a minute to bring the session up after graphical.target (cold first boot on a slow SD card) leaves the appliance dark until a manual `systemctl reset-failed` — with the only documented remedy being one that does nothing.\n\n## Failure scenario\nFresh install, systemd unit enabled, slow SD card: boot reaches graphical.target, service starts, pygame gets \"No available video device\"/connection refused, exits; retries at +10s, +20s, +30s, +40s all race the still-starting session; at the fifth failure the unit hits start-limit-hit and stays failed. Operator finds the documented troubleshooting entry, adds After=graphical-session.target, reloads, reboots — identical behavior, because the directive is ignored. There is no working documented path out of the race.\n\n## Suggested fix\nFix the doc and the unit together: either (a) run the app as a systemd *user* service (where After=graphical-session.target is real and the session env — DISPLAY/WAYLAND_DISPLAY/XDG_RUNTIME_DIR — is inherited correctly), documenting `loginctl enable-linger pi`; or (b) keep the system unit but make startup robust to the race: `ExecStartPre=/bin/sh -c 'until [ -S /tmp/.X11-unix/X0 ]; do sleep 1; done'` (bounded), or raise RestartSec/StartLimitBurst so five attempts cover a realistic session bring-up window. Delete or correct the graphical-session.target advice in Troubleshooting.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe suggested fixes are sound with two corrections. (1) For option (b), an `ExecStartPre` until-loop is implicitly bounded by TimeoutStartSec (default 90s) — but a pre-start timeout still counts as a failed start against the SAME StartLimitBurst=5, so ExecStartPre alone does not remove the start-limit exposure; pair it with a raised burst (e.g. StartLimitBurst=10) or longer RestartSec. Also, on Raspberry Pi OS Bookworm the default session is Wayland (labwc/wayfire) with Xwayland — /tmp/.X11-unix/X0 appears only when Xwayland is up, which may lag the compositor; the socket poll is still the right signal for this X11/pygame app but the doc should say which OS variant it assumes. The cheapest correct fix preserving STAB-4's intent is simply RestartSec=15 + StartLimitBurst=10 (retries then cover ~2.5 min of session bring-up while a genuinely broken boot still goes dark, just later). (2) Option (a) (user service + enable-linger) is the systemd-idiomatic fix but is a larger change: it invalidates the guide's CRIT-4/STAB-4 [Unit] discussion, the journalctl/systemctl invocations (need --user), and first-boot-checklist's recovery text — budget the doc churn or prefer (b). Either way, the graphical-session.target sentence at :561 must be corrected, not merely supplemented — it is the only race-specific remedy the docs offer and it verifiably does nothing.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\ndocs/pi-setup-guide.md:559-561: \"**App starts but pygame window is invisible** — The service may be starting before the desktop is fully up. Add `After=graphical-session.target` to `[Unit]` in the service file and reload.\" systemd.special(7): graphical-session.target is listed under \"Special Passive User Units\" (\"This target is active whenever any graphical session is running... intended to be used by user units\"); systemd ignores After=/Before= on units that don't exist in the manager. Unit context: :343 `After=network-online.target time-sync.target graphical.target`, :344-345 `StartLimitIntervalSec=300` / `StartLimitBurst=5`, :354-355 `Restart=on-failure` / `RestartSec=10`. The math: failures at t=0,10,20,30,40s exhaust the burst in ~50s. Closed #83 (CRIT-4) covered only the time-sync ordering half of this [Unit] block; the session-race remedy was never audited.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
   "labels": [
    "code-review",
    "documentation",
    "severity:medium"
   ],
   "milestone_title": "Wave 4 — First boot: install path, operator tooling & token hygiene"
  },
  {
   "finding_id": "sec-1",
   "title": "[R4:sec-1] Discogs user token leaks verbatim into persistent logs via discogs_client exception text on any network blip (bypasses _redact_url)",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `sec-1` · **Wave 4 — First boot: install path, operator tooling & token hygiene** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **MEDIUM**_\n\n**Location:** `src/metadata/resolver.py:138`  \n**Lens:** Untrusted input & security\n\n## Defect\nThe S-4/SEC-2 redaction work protects only the app's own REST transport, where the token rides in an Authorization header — transport.py's _redact_url docstring even states 'The auth token rides in a header (never the URL), so this isn't a live leak today'. That premise is false for the OTHER half of Discogs traffic: the python3-discogs-client library used by DiscogsReader (search, release, master, tracklist fetches) authenticates via UserTokenRequestsFetcher, which sends the token as a QUERY PARAMETER on every request (discogs_client/fetchers.py:150, `params={'token': self.user_token}`). When such a request fails at the requests level (DNS failure, connection refused/reset, connect timeout, TLS error — the routine flaky-wifi conditions this codebase explicitly designs for elsewhere, e.g. PCONC-2), the raised requests.exceptions.ConnectionError stringifies as 'Max retries exceeded with url: /database/search?...&token=<RAW TOKEN>'. That exception is then logged VERBATIM, never passing through _redact_url: resolver.py:138 `log.info(f\"Discogs collection search couldn't determine (transient): {e}\")` (also lines 140, 162, 164), and reader.py:206 `log.warning(f\"Failed to fetch tracklist for release {release_id}: {e}\")`. main.py configures level=INFO, so the transient INFO line is emitted, and on the Pi it persists in the systemd journal across reboots (the same sink SEC-3 #114 was filed to protect). The operator script scripts/discogs_live_check.py (lines 47-48, 78-79, 96-97) prints the same exception class raw to the terminal. The token grants full write access to the user's Discogs account. The existing tests (tests/test_discogs_security.py) assert only that _redact_url drops query strings — no test covers the library-exception path, which is the only path where the token actually appears in a URL. This is a new residual, not a re-litigation of closed S-4 (#37: transport log lines), SEC-2 (#120: _redact_url empty-path fallback), or SEC-3 (#114: config-error echo).\n\n## Failure scenario\nNormal 24/7 operation, correctly configured appliance. Wifi/router blips for a few seconds while a new album's first track resolves (or a tracklist fetch is in flight). discogs_client's requests call raises ConnectionError/ConnectTimeout whose message embeds the full request path including `token=<the real Discogs personal-access token>`. resolver.py:138 (INFO) or reader.py:206 (WARNING) writes that message to the systemd journal, where it persists across reboots. The token is later exposed to anyone reading the journal — most plausibly the owner pasting a journalctl excerpt into a GitHub issue, chat, or forum while debugging ('covers stopped loading'), the exact real-world channel tokens leak through. The token allows arbitrary writes to the owner's Discogs collection and account.\n\n## Suggested fix\nDefense-in-depth at the composition root: in main.py, after load_config(), install a logging.Filter on the root logger that scrubs known secrets from every record's rendered message — replace the exact config.discogs.user_token (and lastfm api_key/api_secret/session_key) string with '<redacted>', plus a belt-and-suspenders regex `token=[^&\\s]+` → `token=<redacted>`. This covers all five current sites (resolver.py:138/140/162/164, reader.py:206) and any future log site for free, mirroring how SEC-3 centralized redaction at the boundary rather than per-site. Optionally add a `redact_exc(e)` helper in errors.py for the resolver/reader sites and use it in discogs_live_check.py's `fail(f\"Exception: {e}\")` lines. Correct the now-misleading claim in transport.py's _redact_url docstring ('never the URL') to note the discogs_client half DOES put the token in the URL. Add a regression test that raises a ConnectionError with a token-bearing MaxRetryError message through resolve() under caplog and asserts the token string is absent from every record.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe suggested logging.Filter must be attached to the root HANDLER (the one basicConfig installs), not the root logger: per Python logging semantics, logger-level filters do not apply to records propagating up from child loggers (src.metadata.resolver etc.), so a root-logger filter would scrub nothing from the five cited sites. The filter should scrub the rendered record.getMessage() and then set record.msg = scrubbed / record.args = () so %-style args cannot reintroduce the secret. Rest of the fix (exact-string scrub of all four credentials plus token=[^&\\s]+ regex, redact_exc helper for discogs_live_check.py, docstring correction in transport.py, caplog regression test through resolve()) is sound as proposed.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nLibrary behavior — /usr/local/lib/python3.11/dist-packages/discogs_client/fetchers.py:150: `method, url, data=data, headers=headers, params={'token':self.user_token}`. Log sites — src/metadata/resolver.py:138 `log.info(f\"Discogs collection search couldn't determine (transient): {e}\")` (is_transient(ConnectionError)=True routes here); src/metadata/discogs/reader.py:206 `log.warning(f\"Failed to fetch tracklist for release {release_id}: {e}\")`. main.py:41 `level=logging.INFO`. Executed reproduction (discogs_client.Client with user_token, base URL pointed at a refused port, formatted exactly as resolver.py:138 does): output was `exc type: ConnectionError / is_transient: True / LOGGED LINE: Discogs collection search couldn't determine (transient): HTTPConnectionPool(host='127.0.0.1', port=1): Max retries exceeded with url: /database/search?artist=Miles%20Davis&type=release&q=Kind%20of%20Blue&page=1&per_page=50&token=FAKE_DISCOGS_TOKEN_hunter2hunter2 (...) / TOKEN IN LOGGED MESSAGE: True`. Prior-round coverage gap: tests/test_discogs_security.py tests only _redact_url (lines 45-84); transport.py:73-75 docstring asserts the token is 'never the URL'.\n\n</details>\n\n---\nRelated: the gap2-2 issue is the same token-leak family (discogs_client query-parameter auth) surfacing in the operator script; one logging-filter fix at the composition root covers the runtime half.\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
