  {
   "finding_id": "gap2-2",
   "title": "[R4:gap2-2] discogs_live_check.py prints the Discogs user token verbatim on library-half network errors — discogs_client 2.9 carries the token in the URL query and fail() echoes raw exception text",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `gap2-2` · **Wave 4 — First boot: install path, operator tooling & token hygiene** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **LOW**_\n\n**Location:** `scripts/discogs_live_check.py:48`  \n**Lens:** Gap: Unowned operator tooling and test harness (discogs_live_check.py, conftest stub, factories, get_lastfm_session_key runtime)\n\n## Defect\nChecks 1-3 route through python3-discogs-client (reader._database_search / _client.release), whose UserTokenRequestsFetcher sends the token as a URL query parameter (`params={'token': self.user_token}` — verified in installed discogs_client 2.9), unlike DiscogsHttp which uses an Authorization header. A requests transport error propagates uncaught out of the library into the script's `except Exception as e: fail(f\"Exception: {e}\")` (lines 47-48, 78-79, 96-97), and requests embeds the full path+query in ConnectionError text. The same text also reaches stderr via reader.get_tracklist's log.warning through the unconfigured-logging lastResort handler. This is the terminal-scrollback sink the project's own S-3 (#36) treated as a leak worth fixing, and the sibling of the already-filed resolver→journal leak — but fixing the resolver's logging will not fix this script's print().\n\n## Failure scenario\nOperator runs the bring-up check on a Pi whose Wi-Fi drops mid-run (first boot is exactly when the network is flaky). Check 1 prints: '✗ Exception: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='api.discogs.com', ...): Max retries exceeded with url: /database/search?...&token=<their real Discogs token> ...' — the write-capable token lands in terminal scrollback, tmux/SSH session logs, and any screen recording of the setup session.\n\n## Suggested fix\nAdd a small `_redact(text)` helper to the script that masks `token=[^&\\s]+` (and optionally the username path segment, reusing transport._redact_url's approach) and route every `fail(f\"Exception: {e}\")` through it. Also call logging.basicConfig with a redacting filter or at least note the exposure.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe proposed _redact(text) helper masking token=[^&\\s]+ on every fail(f\"Exception: {e}\") path is correct and sufficient for the print sink. Two refinements: (a) reusing transport._redact_url alone would NOT work here — the URL is embedded inside requests' prose exception message, so a whole-message regex is the right tool; _redact_url is only useful as the model for also masking the /users/<name>/ segment. (b) Also add logging.basicConfig with a redacting logging.Filter (or route the script's logging through the same _redact) because reader.get_tracklist swallows the exception and log.warning's str(e) to stderr via the lastResort handler — redacting only the fail() calls leaves that second sink open. Alternatively, the root fix is to stop the token riding in the URL at all by giving the library a header-auth fetcher, but that changes production transport behavior and is out of proportion for a LOW operator-tooling finding.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nInstalled discogs_client 2.9 UserTokenRequestsFetcher.fetch: `resp = self.request(method, url, data=data, headers=headers, params={'token':self.user_token})`. Executed repro of the exact print path: `requests.get('https://api.discogs.com.invalid/database/search', params={'q':'Sister','token':'SECRET_TOKEN_ABC123'}, timeout=3)` → `Exception: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='api.discogs.com.invalid', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: /database/search?q=Sister&token=SECRET_TOKEN_ABC123 (...)`; 'token in printed text: True'. Script lines 47-48/78-79/96-97 print `f\"Exception: {e}\"` unredacted.\n\n</details>\n\n---\nRelated: the sec-1 issue is the runtime half of the same token-leak family.\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
