  {
   "finding_id": "disp-1",
   "title": "[R4:disp-1] Status-strip labels are muted-on-solid-surface, brighter than the DISP-2 clamp target — WCAG 4.5:1 fails on nearly all covers (measured 3.56–4.44:1)",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `disp-1` · **Wave 5 — Display correctness** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **MEDIUM**_\n\n**Location:** `src/display/renderer.py:755`  \n**Lens:** Display, palette & WCAG guarantee\n\n## Defect\nThe DISP-1/DISP-2 fixes clamp `muted` and `accent` against `text_background(bg, surface)` = bg + 0.55·(surface−bg), documented as \"the brightest colour the card gradient can put under text\" (palette.py:184-193). But the status strip is filled with SOLID `p.surface` (renderer.py:755: `pygame.draw.rect(target, p.surface, (strip.x, strip.y, strip.w, strip.h))`), which is strictly brighter than that clamp target (surface > tb whenever surface > bg), and both strip texts — the state label (renderer.py:760) and the side counter \"SIDE A · 04 OF 06\" (renderer.py:764) — are rendered in `p.muted` directly on it. `ensure_contrast(muted, tb, 4.5)` (palette.py:318) stops lifting the moment 4.5:1 is reached against tb, so against the brighter strip the guarantee is systematically short. The lerp re-clamp (palette_transition.py:76-78) has the same blind spot, and the invariant tests (tests/test_palette_contrast.py:65-76) only ever assert against tb, so nothing notices. The empty-state labels drawn on solid `p.surface` cover fills (renderer.py:1026-1056) share the root cause but settle on FALLBACK_PALETTE where muted-vs-surface is 4.83:1, so only the now-playing strip violation persists for the whole track.\n\n## Failure scenario\nPlay any album whose dominant cover colour is mid-gray or brighter (white/cream sleeves, bright pop art — the majority of real covers). extract_palette produces e.g. bg=(56,56,56), surface=(89,89,89), muted=(191,189,186): muted passes 4.73:1 against tb=(74,74,74) but the strip labels 'NOW PLAYING' and 'SIDE A · 04 OF 06' actually sit on (89,89,89) and measure 3.74:1 — below WCAG AA — for the entire duration of the record. Executed sweep over grayscale dominants: 11/16 levels fail (3.74–4.44:1); pure white 3.74, cream 3.78, bright yellow 3.74, light gray 3.98, mid gray 4.08. Only genuinely dark covers pass.\n\n## Suggested fix\nClamp text roles against the brightest surface text actually lands on, which is `surface` itself, not the gradient peak: since surface ≥ tb always, replacing the clamp target with `surface` (in extract_palette:318, the accent clamp at :302 only if accent ever lands on the strip — currently the dot is non-text so 3:1 suffices and passes, so accent can stay on tb) and in _quantize_palette (palette_transition.py:77) subsumes the DISP-2 guarantee in one move. Alternatively keep gradient fidelity and derive a separate strip-muted role clamped against `surface`, used by _draw_header and the empty-state cover labels. Extend tests/test_palette_contrast.py to assert muted-vs-surface ≥ 4.5 (the assertion that would have caught this).\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nClamping muted against surface instead of tb does subsume the tb guarantee (surface ≥ tb and post-clamp muted is far lighter than surface), but it brightens secondary text everywhere including the gradient card — a visible design change Lane should approve first; the separate strip-muted role is the lower-risk alternative. The empty-state carve-out is only safe if BOOT/ERROR always render FALLBACK_PALETTE; if the palette lerps from a prior track's palette into an error state, renderer.py:1026-1056 shows the same muted-on-solid-surface pattern and can violate transiently, so the fix should cover those labels too. Agreed that the accent dot is non-text (3:1) and can stay clamped on tb. Add the muted-vs-surface ≥ 4.5 assertion to tests/test_palette_contrast.py AND to the _quantize_palette lerp path, since both share the blind spot.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nrenderer.py:755 `pygame.draw.rect(target, p.surface, (strip.x, strip.y, strip.w, strip.h))` + :760/:764 labels rendered in p.muted. palette.py:184 docstring: \"Brightest colour the card gradient can put under text\". Executed with the real extract_palette on synthesized covers: pure white → bg=(56,56,56) surface=(89,89,89) tb=(74,74,74) muted=(191,189,186); muted vs tb 4.73 (guarantee met) but muted vs surface 3.74 (strip). Sweep: 11/16 grayscale dominant levels (80–240) fail, range 3.74–4.44:1. tests/test_palette_contrast.py asserts only contrast_ratio(pal.muted, tb) — never vs pal.surface.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
   "labels": [
    "code-review",
    "bug",
    "severity:medium"
   ],
   "milestone_title": "Wave 5 — Display correctness"
  },
  {
   "finding_id": "arch-6",
   "title": "[R4:arch-6] Renderer _spawn's done callback never retrieves task exceptions — escaping OSErrors (e.g. worn-SD stat/unlink) surface only as detached GC-time 'Task exception was never retrieved', unlike the CONC-3-fixed tracker registry",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `arch-6` · **Wave 5 — Display correctness** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **LOW**_\n\n**Location:** `src/display/renderer.py:434`  \n**Lens:** Architecture drift & tech debt\n\n## Defect\nThe codebase now contains two hand-rolled 'strong-ref set + done callback' registries for fire-and-forget tasks. ListenTracker's (#105/CONC-3 fix) retrieves task.exception() in its done callback and logs a failure attached to its cause (listen_tracker.py:124-158). DisplayRenderer._spawn — written later, with its own DISP-8 no-running-loop guard — registers only `self._bg_tasks.discard` (renderer.py:434), so an exception escaping a display background task (a path not covered by the coroutines' internal try/excepts — e.g. a raise from _cover_store.path_for on a malformed URL, or a future edit to _prefetch_cover) surfaces only as asyncio's detached 'Task exception was never retrieved' message at GC time, the exact failure mode the CONC-3 fix names and repairs on the tracker side. This is the task-registry analogue of closed A-11 (#15, two observer implementations with divergent exception policies, unified into Signal): the same class of divergence has regrown one abstraction over.\n\n## Failure scenario\nA future edit to _decode_cover_async introduces a raise outside its inner try blocks (e.g. in the finally, or the inflight bookkeeping). Covers silently stop updating; the journal shows only an unattributed 'Task exception was never retrieved' from the GC at an arbitrary later time, with none of the URL context the tracker-side pattern would have logged — a debugging session on the Pi instead of one grep.\n\n## Suggested fix\nExtract src/util/tasks.py: `spawn(coro, registry, *, log, label)` combining the DISP-8 no-loop guard, the strong-ref set, and a done callback that discards, skips cancelled, and logs a retrieved exception with the label. Use it from both _spawn and on_silence_event; both existing behaviors are preserved and the exception policy stops diverging.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe proposed shared `spawn(coro, registry, *, log, label)` is sound for the renderer side, but two behavior-preservation caveats on unifying with `on_silence_event`: (1) the tracker's done callback carries a deliberately domain-specific operator message (CONC-3/#163/#171: \"Play Count / Last Played and any Last.fm love may be incomplete, and nothing will retry it\") — a generic label would lose that, so the shared util should accept a custom on-exception callback or the tracker should keep its bespoke callback and share only the mechanics; (2) the DISP-8 no-loop guard degrades to a silent no-op, which is correct for a display repaint but NOT obviously correct for a SESSION_ENDED credit — today the tracker would raise loudly on no loop; silently dropping a credit is data-loss-adjacent, so if the guard is included for the tracker path it must at minimum log.error, or the guard should be opt-in per call site. Also, a cheaper interim fix (no extraction) is a one-line change: replace `self._bg_tasks.discard` with a small callback that discards, skips cancelled, and logs — matching CONC-3 without the refactor.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nrenderer.py:432-434 `task = asyncio.create_task(coro); self._bg_tasks.add(task); task.add_done_callback(self._bg_tasks.discard)` — no exception retrieval. listen_tracker.py:128-133 (same pattern's done callback) exists precisely because a raise otherwise surfaces 'as asyncio's detached ``Task exception was never retrieved`` warning from the GC at an arbitrary later time (or, if the task object is collected quietly, nothing at all)' — the renderer registry still has that documented defect.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
   "labels": [
    "code-review",
    "bug",
    "severity:low"
   ],
   "milestone_title": "Wave 5 — Display correctness"
  },
  {
   "finding_id": "disp-2",
   "title": "[R4:disp-2] Static screens settle on the quantized lerp palette — final exact-target frame never rendered (IDLE/ERROR, and now-playing under reduced_motion)",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `disp-2` · **Wave 5 — Display correctness** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **LOW**_\n\n**Location:** `src/display/palette_transition.py:154`  \n**Lens:** Display, palette & WCAG guarantee\n\n## Defect\nThis is a compositional bug between two prior fixes: P-4 (quantize the in-flight lerp palette to 16-steps per channel so cache keys stay stable) and the static-frame optimisation (the render loop goes quiet when nothing animates). `PaletteTransition.animated()` returns the exact target only when its own elapsed ≥ 1.0s (palette_transition.py:149-151); every frame before that is `_quantize_palette(...)` — channels floored toward black in steps of 16. The render loop (renderer.py:530-541) re-renders only while `dirty or transitioning`; on IDLE/ERROR `_render_empty` sets dirty only when `spec.animates` (renderer.py:998-999, False for both), and now-playing sets it only when `not reduced_motion` (renderer.py:599-600). So the last frame ever rendered is drawn at t just under 1.0 with the QUANTIZED palette, then `transitioning` flips False and the loop goes quiet — the exact-target frame is never composed. The window in which the loop's <1.0 check and animated()'s ≥1.0 check straddle is ~1ms of a 33ms frame, so ~97% of transitions into a static screen settle wrong.\n\n## Failure scenario\nLift the needle → state goes IDLE → 1s lerp toward FALLBACK_PALETTE. Executed simulation of the run() loop: last drawn frame has bg=(0,0,0) instead of (10,10,10), surface=(16,16,16) instead of (22,22,22), text=(224,224,208) instead of (235,230,220) — and the screen holds that wrong frame for the entire idle period (potentially hours on a 24/7 appliance) until the next state change. Same for the ERROR screen and, with display.reduced_motion=true, for every now-playing frame after each track's 1s lerp. Readability is not broken (the quantize path re-clamps muted/accent against the quantized tb) — the defect is a permanently wrong rendition of the specified palette, including a full-black background the design deliberately avoids (bg floor is 8-10, never 0).\n\n## Suggested fix\nDraw one final frame after the transition ends: in run(), track the previous `transitioning` value and set `self._dirty = True` on the True→False edge (one-line fix), so the last composed frame uses the exact target palette (animated() then returns target and snaps current). Alternatively have animated() clamp t so the last quantized frame is only ever produced while a following exact frame is guaranteed — but the loop-edge dirty flag is simpler and testable without pygame.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe proposed True→False edge-detect in run() is correct: on the edge iteration elapsed >= 1.0, so animated() returns the exact target and snaps current; initialize prev_transitioning = False (the startup dirty=True covers the first frame). An equally simple variant with no new state: set self._dirty = True inside the transitioning branch, guaranteeing exactly one post-transition render. Either way, the regression test must be loop-level (simulate the dirty-or-transitioning loop against PaletteTransition, no pygame needed — see /tmp/repro_disp2.py) — the existing settle tests (test_animated_settles_to_exact_target, test_animated_palette_settles_to_exact_target) only exercise animated() and stay green on the broken code.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nExecuted repro (simulating run()'s render-while-transitioning loop at 30fps against the real PaletteTransition): target bg/surface/text (10,10,10)/(22,22,22)/(235,230,220); final on-glass frame (0,0,0)/(16,16,16)/(224,224,208); `frame equals target? False`. Code path: palette_transition.py:147-156 (quantized until elapsed ≥ _TRANSITION_SECS), renderer.py:530-531 (`transitioning = (time.monotonic() - self._transition_start) < _TRANSITION_SECS`; render only `if self._dirty or transitioning`), renderer.py:998-999 (`if spec.animates: self._dirty = True` — False for IDLE/ERROR), renderer.py:599-600 (`if not self.reduced_motion: self._dirty = True`).\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
   "labels": [
    "code-review",
    "bug",
    "severity:low"
   ],
