   "finding_id": "test-1",
   "title": "[R4:test-1] run()'s chunk-dispatch (silence.process/recognizer.enqueue) is unpinned: a mutant dropping every chunk passes 948/948 — residual of the #138/TQ-7 remediation",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `test-1` · **Wave 6 — Test debt, mutation pins & doc drift** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **MEDIUM**_\n\n**Location:** `src/audio/capture.py:361`  \n**Lens:** Test-suite & docs quality\n\n## Defect\nCoverage shows capture.py lines 359-365 never execute: the nominal-path body of run()'s block loop — `for chunk in assembler.feed(block): self.silence.process(chunk, self.sample_rate); await self.recognizer.enqueue(chunk, self.sample_rate)` — is the single integration point that feeds the ENTIRE pipeline (silence detection → SESSION_ENDED → Play Count credit; recognizer → display/scrobble). The #138 (TQ-7 'AudioCapture.run() fully uncovered') remediation added tests for run()'s failure paths — the CONC-5 stall raise (346-358) and the #164 retry/backoff (366-370) are now covered — but the happy path was omitted, and this exact region was just rewritten again for #164 in v1.5.7. ChunkAssembler and SilenceDetector are unit-tested in isolation, but nothing pins that run() actually calls them per block.\n\n## Failure scenario\nAny future refactor of run()'s inner loop (a likely event — it was restructured for #164 days ago) that drops or reorders the silence.process/recognizer.enqueue calls ships with the suite fully green. On the Pi the appliance boots, shows LISTENING forever, never recognizes a track, never starts a session, never credits a Play Count, never scrobbles — total silent loss of all user data the product exists to record, discoverable only by a human noticing the display never changes.\n\n## Suggested fix\nAdd a run()-level test (pattern already exists in the CONC-5 stall tests): start run() with a stubbed InputStream, push one real audio block through the queue, and assert (a) silence.process received the assembled chunk with the right sample_rate and (b) recognizer.enqueue was awaited with the same chunk. Also cover the `block is None` stop-sentinel `continue` at 359-360.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe suggested test (start run() with a stubbed InputStream, push a block via cap._blocks, assert both dispatch calls) is the right pattern, with three cautions: (a) make_capture() builds AudioCapture(config, MagicMock(), MagicMock()) — the recognizer mock must be an AsyncMock (or recognizer.enqueue = AsyncMock()), because run() awaits enqueue and awaiting a plain MagicMock's return value raises TypeError; the existing stall tests never hit this because no block ever flows. (b) Beware a vacuously-green test: ChunkAssembler only emits once it has chunk_frames of audio — at the default chunk_seconds=15 / 44100 Hz that is 661,500 frames, far more than one _BLOCK_SECONDS block. Either push enough frames or construct the capture with a small chunk_seconds; the positive assertions on the mocks (`process.assert_called()` / `enqueue.assert_awaited()`) are what guard against feeding too little and passing anyway. (c) Wait for cap._blocks to be non-None (run() assigns it after the retry-loop entry) before put_nowait, as the stall tests already do implicitly via events. Also assert the sample_rate argument on both calls, and add the cheap `block is None` sentinel case (put None while _running stays True, assert neither mock fires) — that covers line 359-360 too.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\npytest --cov: 'src/audio/capture.py 133 9 93% 228, 359-365, 374-376'. Executed mutation in a /tmp copy: replaced the dispatch body with `for chunk in assembler.feed(block): pass  # MUTANT: chunks silently dropped` → `python3 -m pytest -q` → '948 passed, 1 warning in 5.19s'.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
   "labels": [
    "code-review",
