   "finding_id": "test-6",
   "title": "[R4:test-6] testing-guide.md:104 claims whole suite runs \"in well under a second\"; measured 5.15s at 948 tests",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `test-6` · **Wave 6 — Test debt, mutation pins & doc drift** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **NIT**_\n\n**Location:** `docs/testing-guide.md:104`  \n**Lens:** Test-suite & docs quality\n\n## Defect\ndocs/testing-guide.md:103-104: 'this automatically picks up everything in the tests/ directory and runs it in well under a second (no I/O, no hardware)'. Measured on this audit: 6.10s and 5.02s plain, 9.52s with coverage, at 948 tests. The claim was presumably true at a few hundred tests and was never recomputed — ironic three lines above the section that deliberately un-pins the test COUNT because 'it drifts every time a suite is added' (T-8). The per-TEST 'well under a second' claims in pytest.ini and testing-guide.md:158 remain true; only the whole-suite sentence is stale.\n\n## Failure scenario\nAn operator on the Pi (slower than this sandbox) sees a 20-30s run, concludes something is hung per the doc's expectation, and interrupts a healthy suite — or, per this project's own compute-don't-type rule, the number is simply wrong in a doc that just legislated against typed counts.\n\n## Suggested fix\nChange to 'in a few seconds' (or drop the duration claim entirely, matching the T-8 treatment of the count).\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nSuggested fix is correct and minimal: change \"in well under a second\" to \"in a few seconds\" or drop the duration entirely (consistent with the T-8 treatment of the count three lines below). If keeping a number, prefer an order-of-magnitude phrase (\"a few seconds on a laptop, tens of seconds on the Pi\") over a typed measurement that will drift again. Leave line 158's per-test claim untouched — it is still accurate and is load-bearing for the pytest-timeout rationale.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\ndocs/testing-guide.md:104 'runs it in well under a second (no I/O, no hardware)'. Executed: `python3 -m pytest -q` → '948 passed, 1 warning in 6.10s' (5.02s on a second run; 9.52s under --cov).\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
