  {
   "finding_id": "arch-1",
   "title": "[R4:arch-1] Commit-path epoch invariant is ~6 hand-placed point checks with a 5-issue recurrence history; no structural guard for the new awaits v1.6/v1.7 will add",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `arch-1` · **Wave 7 — Architecture pay-down & roadmap seams** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (verified by code reading) · severity **MEDIUM**_\n\n**Location:** `src/app/track_commit_service.py:105`  \n**Lens:** Architecture drift & tech debt\n\n## Defect\nThe rule 'after any await in the commit path, re-validate the audio's session epoch before the next side effect' is the single most audit-expensive invariant in this codebase, and it is implemented as eight hand-placed point checks rather than a structure that carries it. Sites: PlayerState.session_epoch (player_state.py:59), epoch bind at enqueue (recognizer.py:319), the recognizer's _last_epoch health-counter reset (recognizer.py:434-437) and _pending_epoch void (recognizer.py:453-455), four separate comparisons inside TrackCommitService.commit (lines 105, 120's is_stale lambda, 138, 152), and the tracker's post-lock is_stale re-check (listen_tracker.py:541). Five separate closed findings across three rounds — B-1 (#1), PCONC-1 (#80), B-19 (#68), LB-1 (#84), CONC-6 (#87) — were each an instance of ONE await missing ONE re-check. The pattern is not done recurring: v1.6 (write a play-history entry at commit) and v1.7 (persist side-completion state) both add new awaits exactly into this path, and each will need its own manually remembered guard or it re-opens the class.\n\n## Failure scenario\nA v1.6/v1.7 implementer adds `await history.append(metadata)` after `set_track` in commit() (the natural place). The needle lifts during that disk write; the subsequent scrobble/set_raw guards still hold, but the new step itself commits into a dead session — the exact B-19/LB-1 shape, sixth occurrence — and nothing structural flagged that the new await needed a check.\n\n## Suggested fix\nIntroduce a tiny guard object: `guard = state.epoch_guard(audio_epoch)` with `guard.still_current()` (and optionally `guard.run(step_coro)` that awaits and returns None-if-stale). Thread ONE guard through commit() and pass it (not a lambda) to on_track_identified, replacing the four inline comparisons. The recognizer's enqueue-time bind stays; the payoff is that any new commit-path step composes with the guard instead of re-deriving the comparison, and the invariant has one named home a reviewer can point at.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe proposed guard object improves discoverability (one named, greppable home; collapses four duplicate comment blocks) but does NOT structurally enforce the invariant — a v1.6 implementer can still add an await and forget to call guard.still_current(), exactly as before. To actually close the class, make guard.run(step) the only sanctioned way to add a commit-path side effect: restructure commit() as a sequence of guard-wrapped steps so an unwrapped await is visible as a pattern violation in review, and document in the guard's docstring that every new side effect goes through run(). Two implementation cautions: (1) the tracker's CONC-6 semantics require the staleness predicate to be re-evaluated AFTER the lifecycle lock is acquired — passing guard.still_current (bound method) instead of the lambda preserves this, since it reads state.session_epoch live, but a fix that pre-computes staleness before the lock re-opens #87; (2) keep the enqueue-time bind in the recognizer as-is (PCONC-1 depends on capture-time binding), and leave the recognizer's _last_epoch/_pending_epoch sites alone — they are different invariants (per-session health reset, pending-candidate tagging), not commit-path checks, and folding them into the guard would conflate two mechanisms.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\ntrack_commit_service.py:105 `if self.state.session_epoch != audio_epoch:`; :120 `is_stale=lambda: self.state.session_epoch != audio_epoch`; :138 `if self.state.session_epoch == audio_epoch:`; :152 `if self.lastfm and self.state.session_epoch == audio_epoch:`. Each carries its own multi-line comment re-explaining the same rule (B-11/LB-1/B-19 blocks, lines 97-103, 122-137, 145-151). recognizer.py:319 `epoch = self.state.session_epoch  # Bind the session epoch...(PCONC-1)`. Five closed issues (#1, #68, #80, #84, #87) are each one missed instance of this rule.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
   "labels": [
    "code-review",
