  {
   "finding_id": "arch-2",
   "title": "[R4:arch-2] No persistence layer exists yet for v1.6/v1.7 roadmap needs, and the finalize path's latch-guard pattern is hand-mirrored twice (future third copy risk) — real but zero present runtime impact",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `arch-2` · **Wave 7 — Architecture pay-down & roadmap seams** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (executed reproduction) · severity **LOW**_\n\n**Location:** `src/tracking/listen_tracker.py:285`  \n**Lens:** Architecture drift & tech debt\n\n## Defect\nv1.6 needs a 'grid of recently played album covers (pulled from session history or a small local log)' (docs/roadmap.md:400-402) and v1.7 needs side-completion state 'stored in a small local state file between sessions (e.g. ~/.vinyl-now-playing/session-state.json)' (roadmap.md:423-424). Neither data source exists anywhere: PlayerState is documented 'in-memory only, no persistence between reboots' (player_state.py:3), a grep for json.dump/json.load/write_text over src/ returns ZERO hits (the only disk writer in the app is the cover cache's binary files), and a finalized PlaySession simply goes out of scope at the end of _finalize_session — its identified_tracks, release id and timestamps are discarded, so even in-memory there is no history. This is distinct from #169 (Discogs index persistence, a deliberately declined efficiency cache); the roadmap features REQUIRE durable app state as a correctness input. Second cost in the same method: the credited/crediting and loved/loving latch pairs (models.py:301-321) with their retry blocks (listen_tracker.py:306-341 vs 353-386) are already two near-verbatim copies of one 'in-flight latch → bounded-retry write → commit latch on success' pattern; v1.7's 'record side completed' write is a third side effect on this exact path and will be a third copy, with the #163-class subtleties (latch-before-await, commit-after-landing) re-derived by hand.\n\n## Failure scenario\nv1.6 work starts and immediately stalls on architecture questions with no prepared answer: where is play history recorded (finalize? commit?), what survives the crash-loop restarts Wave 2 hardened against, and how does a write from the fire-and-forget finalize task interact with drain()'s 10s bound. Each answered ad hoc inside ListenTracker grows the exact god-method/latch sprawl three audit rounds just spent shrinking, and v1.7 then re-answers them differently for session-state.json.\n\n## Suggested fix\nBefore v1.6: add src/state/session_log.py — an append-only JSONL play log (atomic append, bounded rotation, clock_is_trustworthy-aware timestamps) with one write call invoked from _finalize_session and one read API (last N plays). v1.7's side-completion store becomes a second small consumer of the same file-handling core. Simultaneously extract the latch+retry duo into a helper (e.g. a FinalizeAction holding its own in-flight/committed pair around _finalize_write_with_retry) so the third side effect is a declaration, not a third hand-copied block.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nTwo corrections to the suggested fix. First, the duplication claim is overstated: the bounded-retry logic is ALREADY extracted into _finalize_write_with_retry and shared by both paths — what is duplicated is only the ~8-line latch scaffolding (check committed+in-flight → set in-flight → await write → commit on success), and the two blocks are analogous rather than near-verbatim (the credit path wraps a two-write helper with #171 exception containment; the love path calls the retry helper directly). So the proposed FinalizeAction helper should wrap only the latch pair around the existing retry helper, not rebuild the retry. Second, timing: session_log.py need not land \"before v1.6\" — building it IS naturally the first task of v1.6 (and the latch-helper extraction the first task of v1.7's side-completion work). File it as a roadmap-attached issue so the #163 subtleties are extracted rather than re-derived, not as pre-work blocking the current release.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nGrep `json\\.dump|json\\.load|\\.write_text|open\\(.*'w` over src/ → 'No matches found'. player_state.py:3 'Intentionally simple: in-memory only, no persistence between reboots.' roadmap.md:402 'pulled from session history or a small local log' and :424 'stored in a small local state file between sessions'. listen_tracker.py:353-386 is structurally the same block as :306-341 with credited/crediting renamed to loved/loving; models.py:309-321 documents the two latch pairs as deliberate mirrors ('the love-side analogue of the B-8 credited/crediting guard').\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
   "labels": [
    "code-review",
    "architecture",
    "severity:low"
   ],
   "milestone_title": "Wave 7 — Architecture pay-down & roadmap seams"
  },
  {
   "finding_id": "arch-3",
   "title": "[R4:arch-3] v1.6 idle-screen pre-work: land seams first — idle-is-static invariant, an idle-time cover-fetch entry point, and an app-layer idle-content feed (no current defect)",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `arch-3` · **Wave 7 — Architecture pay-down & roadmap seams** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (verified by code reading) · severity **LOW**_\n\n**Location:** `src/display/renderer.py:246`  \n**Lens:** Architecture drift & tech debt\n\n## Defect\nThe next release (v1.6) turns the idle screen into a recent-covers grid with an optional clock and a Discogs random pick (roadmap.md:399-405). Three current structural facts each fight that directly. (1) Idle is hard-coded fully static: _EMPTY_STATES[IDLE] has animates=False and the design doctrine 'idle and error are fully static so the render loop goes quiet' is baked into _render_empty's keep-awake logic (renderer.py:996-999) and the static-frame key (line 978), which has no time component — a clock or a periodically-refreshed grid needs a minute-tick invalidation concept that doesn't exist. (2) The cover machinery is single-current-cover: _wanted_cover_url is one Optional[str] (line 408), _on_state_change prefetches ONLY on PLAYING (lines 494-506) and explicitly nulls the wanted cover on IDLE (lines 507-511), and the blacklist-lift logic keys off 'the new current track'. A grid needs N thumbnail-sized covers fetched and decoded WHILE idle, which today has no entry point. (3) The idle content itself (recent plays, random collection item) is data the display layer cannot obtain: the renderer reads only PlayerState, which carries no history, and reaching into DiscogsReader from src/display would create the display→metadata dependency the A-4/A-9 refactors spent two rounds removing. Note this is not re-litigating ARCH-3's documented FramePainter deferral — that deferral was scoped on testability grounds before v1.6 was next in line; the new fact is that the next feature lands squarely in the deferred region.\n\n## Failure scenario\nThe v1.6 implementer hangs a DiscogsReader off DisplayRenderer to fetch the random pick (reintroducing the reach-through Law-of-Demeter shape of closed A-3), bolts a per-second dirty flag onto idle (silently repealing the 'idle goes quiet' power/CPU property the Pi relies on 20+ hours a day), and duplicates the prefetch/blacklist/inflight cover plumbing for thumbnails — three regressions of previously-audited properties in one feature.\n\n## Suggested fix\nLand the seams first, as their own patch: (a) an app-layer IdleContentProvider (fed by the v1.6 session log and DiscogsReader) that publishes an IdleContent value into PlayerState (or a sibling view-model Signal) so the renderer stays a pure state reader; (b) generalize the cover fetch entry point to accept a set of (url, size) wants independent of current_track, reusing the existing per-url inflight/blacklist sets which are already shape-compatible; (c) make idle refresh explicit — an idle_tick cadence in the _EmptyStateSpec (animates becomes an enum: static / slow-tick / animated) so the static-frame key can incorporate a time bucket deliberately instead of via a repealed invariant.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe suggested fix is directionally sound but over-scopes (b) and under-credits existing structure. (b) The per-url plumbing already generalizes: _prefetch_cover is per-url with its own _cover_prefetch_inflight dedup (:1346-1348), _cover_bad_urls is a per-url set, CoverArtCache is url-keyed, and _load_cover/_decode_cover_async are already keyed by (url, w, h) (:1418, :1425) — thumbnail sizes need no new machinery. The ONLY missing piece is the entry point: a way to request fetches for a set of urls outside the PLAYING branch. Moreover, the palette side needs NO generalization at all — DESIGN.md:220 mandates the idle screen use the fallback palette, so _wanted_cover_url/_queue_palette can stay single-target; grid thumbnails need decode, not palettes. (c) is right, and cheaper than implied: boot's ticking label already demonstrates the label-term-in-static-key invalidation pattern (:976-978); a clock needs only a coarse time bucket in the key plus a scheduled dirty flip (e.g. a once-a-minute _spawn'd sleeper), never a hot loop — make the enum static/slow-tick/animated as proposed. (a) should note that IdleContent published via PlayerState or a sibling Signal must honor the A-12 event-loop-thread-only contract and the non-blocking-listener rule (player_state.py docstring), since Discogs fetches for the random pick must complete off-callback before publishing. Minor citation fix: the file is docs/roadmap.md (v1.6 section ~lines 389-405), not roadmap.md:399-405.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nrenderer.py:246-253 `EmptyState.IDLE: _EmptyStateSpec(... animates=False)`; :73 'Idle and error are fully static frames — the render loop goes quiet'; :978 idle static key `(\"empty\", state, boot_label, p.bg, ...)` contains no time term; :507-511 `elif state.status in (IDLE, ERROR, LISTENING): self._wanted_cover_url = None; self._queue_palette(None)` — the only cover-prefetch trigger is the PLAYING branch at :494-506. roadmap.md:400-405 lists grid + clock + random record + display.idle_mode.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
   "labels": [
    "code-review",
    "architecture",
    "severity:low"
   ],
   "milestone_title": "Wave 7 — Architecture pay-down & roadmap seams"
  },
  {
   "finding_id": "arch-4",
   "title": "[R4:arch-4] Bounded LRU cache implemented twice (renderer._BoundedCache + hand-rolled copy in MetadataResolver), already subtly diverged on replace semantics",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `arch-4` · **Wave 7 — Architecture pay-down & roadmap seams** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (verified by code reading) · severity **LOW**_\n\n**Location:** `src/metadata/resolver.py:78`  \n**Lens:** Architecture drift & tech debt\n\n## Defect\nrenderer.py:275-307 defines _BoundedCache ('Pure Python, no pygame dependency — unit-tested in tests/test_renderer_caches.py') and the renderer uses it for six caches. MetadataResolver re-implements the identical strategy inline — _cache_get does the pop/re-insert LRU refresh (resolver.py:78-85) and _cache_store does the evict-oldest-past-cap loop (resolver.py:87-92), with the same 'dict preserves insertion order' comment. Two copies of one eviction algorithm means a future fix (e.g. adding a TTL, or the get-on-miss-returns-None vs KeyError contract) lands in one and not the other. A shared, already-tested class exists; it just lives in the wrong module to be imported by src/metadata (importing renderer from resolver would invert layering, which is presumably why the copy happened).\n\n## Failure scenario\nA change to eviction behavior (say v1.6 wants LFU-ish retention for palette entries, or a bug is found in the refresh-on-get ordering) is fixed in _BoundedCache; the resolver's album cache silently keeps the old behavior, and the divergence is invisible because the resolver copy has no shared tests.\n\n## Suggested fix\nMove _BoundedCache to src/util/cache.py (it is dependency-free by design), re-export from renderer for compatibility, and replace resolver's _cache_get/_cache_store internals with an instance (`self._album_cache = BoundedCache(_ALBUM_CACHE_MAX)`). ~20 lines deleted, one algorithm, one test home.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nSuggested fix is viable: src/util/ already exists (clock.py, signal.py), _BoundedCache is dependency-free, and its get-returns-None contract is safe for the resolver (entries are always non-None (source, payload) tuples). When consolidating, note the put semantics choice explicitly: _BoundedCache.put refreshes a replaced key's eviction position; the resolver's current _cache_store does not (moot in practice since stores only follow misses, but the migration should state which behavior is canonical). Keep the renderer re-export so tests/test_renderer_caches.py and any `from src.display.renderer import _BoundedCache` importers keep resolving, and retain test_resolver.py::test_cache_is_bounded as an integration-level check even after the unit tests move.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\nresolver.py:82-84 `# LRU-ish refresh: pop and re-insert so this entry isn't first to evict.` / `self._album_cache.pop(key); self._album_cache[key] = entry`; :90-92 `while len(self._album_cache) > _ALBUM_CACHE_MAX: self._album_cache.pop(next(iter(self._album_cache)))` — line-for-line the logic of renderer.py:288-301 (`_BoundedCache.get`/`put`).\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
   "labels": [
    "code-review",
    "architecture",
    "severity:low"
   ],
   "milestone_title": "Wave 7 — Architecture pay-down & roadmap seams"
  },
  {
   "finding_id": "arch-5",
   "title": "[R4:arch-5] Three variants of one summarizing-throttle pattern hand-rolled across capture.py (plus two distinct idioms elsewhere); the -inf monotonic-seed subtlety documented twice",
   "body": "_Round 4 cold audit (2026-08-07) · finding `arch-5` · **Wave 7 — Architecture pay-down & roadmap seams** · verdict **CONFIRMED** (verified by code reading) · severity **LOW**_\n\n**Location:** `src/audio/capture.py:241`  \n**Lens:** Architecture drift & tech debt\n\n## Defect\nRounds 2-3 fixed journal/SD-card log floods point-by-point, and each fix hand-rolled its own throttle: (1) capture drop-warn — count + interval, -inf seed (capture.py:94-103, 199-215, PCONC-4); (2) capture error log — count + interval + message-change key, -inf seed (capture.py:105-113, 241-267, #178); (3) recognizer churn — modulo counter (recognizer.py:505, B-21); (4) renderer decode-deferred — episode latch cleared on success (renderer.py:382, 1478-1489, STAB-1); (5) device-lookup dedup — two change-keys (capture.py:123-124, 160-177, #164). Mechanisms 1, 2 and 5 are the same interval/change-key summarizing throttle with different field names, and the non-obvious '-inf seed because Pi CLOCK_MONOTONIC restarts at ~0 on reboot' subtlety is documented twice (capture.py:98-101 and 108-110) because it had to be re-derived twice. Every future always-on log site — v1.6's idle Discogs fetch failures, v1.8's request-error logging — will need copy #6 or become the next flood finding.\n\n## Failure scenario\nv1.8's web leg logs each failed /api request; under a broken reverse-proxy health check that's several lines per second to the SD card — the exact PCONC-4/#178 class, re-created because the throttle pattern lives in comments in capture.py rather than in an importable utility.\n\n## Suggested fix\nExtract src/util/logthrottle.py with one class covering the proven semantics: first-event-immediate (-inf seed), change-key resets, suppressed-count summarizing per interval. Port the two capture throttles and the #164 dedup to it (behavior-preserving, their existing tests pin the semantics); leave the two genuinely different idioms (modulo churn counter, success-cleared latch) alone but documented next to it.\n\n## Verifier's fix note\nThe claim that mechanisms 1, 2, 5 are \"the same throttle with different field names\" overstates: mechanism 1 has no change key, and mechanism 5 has no interval and no suppressed-count — it NEVER re-logs while its key is unchanged, a different contract from periodic summarization. The extracted class must therefore make both the change-key and the interval optional (interval=None = pure dedup for #164; key=None for PCONC-4), or the \"behavior-preserving\" port silently changes #164's never-re-log semantics into periodic re-warning. Port subtlety: capture.py:172 updates _last_match_key on every call including single-match calls (so a config becoming NEWLY ambiguous later still warns) — preserve key-update-on-every-observation, not only-on-log. Leaving the modulo counter (B-21) and the renderer latch (STAB-1) alone is right; the latch is cleared by an unrelated success event, which the throttle class cannot express cleanly.\n\n<details><summary>Evidence</summary>\n\ncapture.py:98-101 '# Seeded to -inf (NOT 0.0)... on the Pi CLOCK_MONOTONIC is uptime-based and resets to ~0 on reboot' and the near-verbatim repeat at :108-110 ('seeded to -inf so the first error always reports, independent of the monotonic epoch'). _log_capture_error (241-267) and the drop-warn block (199-215) differ only by the message-change key; capture.py:160-177 is a third change-key variant.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Source: `CODE_REVIEW_2026-08-07.md` (repo root, gitignored — full report incl. dedup record, lens coverage notes, and the completeness-critic assessment)._",
